var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"feasibility_gate/#Feasibility-before-droop-replacement","page":"Feasibility Gate","text":"Historical artifacts/ paths on this page identify local campaign records, not files bundled with the release. See archive access and the current qualification index.\n\nUse qualify_droop_replacement before accepting a proposed set of fixed droop curves for S1. It checks the calibration point, then the source and candidate models at every required demand factor. All candidate curves are present together. A point that passes each generator separately does not establish that the combined system is feasible.\n\nDecision Evidence Accept replacement?\nfeasible An operating point independently satisfies the original AC equations, exact droops and bounds within the frozen tolerances Yes, if every required condition passes\ncertified_infeasible A replayable global necessary-condition contradiction No\nunresolved Neither a validated point nor a certificate No\n\nFeasibility does not require an optimal objective or a successful optimizer termination code. An iteration-limited result can contain a valid witness. Conversely, LOCALLY_INFEASIBLE, a failed multistart campaign, and a positive local Phase-I objective do not prove that no feasible operating point exists.","title":"Feasibility before droop replacement"},{"category":"section","location":"feasibility_gate/#Contract-and-independent-replay","page":"Feasibility Gate","text":"FeasibilityProblem copies and fingerprints the entire case: topology, availability, demand, generator limits, voltage and thermal limits, exact droop knots, fixed equipment settings, selected equipment bounds, optional branch angle limits and all validation tolerances. Changing any of these requires a new qualification. Mutation of a frozen snapshot raises an error. Solver settings and the search objective are recorded with attempts; they do not change the physical feasible set.\n\nwrite_feasibility_result(path, problem, result) saves the full contract and evidence. read_feasibility_result(path, problem) requires the expected contract, rejects stale scope, and replays the saved witness or certificate before reuse.\n\nTaps and shunts default to their fixed source settings. A witness must include the actual settings of selected equipment. Only selected banks may move; their optimization is continuous susceptance optimization, not discrete switching. The checker reconstructs those settings, then checks AC balance, both branch terminals, voltage, generator P/Q, unavailable generation, exact droop Q, controller P limits, reference angles and supplied branch angle limits.\n\nBranch does not currently store angle-difference limits. Supply source-case limits through angle_limits=Dict(branch_id => (min_radians,max_radians)) when they belong to the required problem. These limits are enforced in the local search as well as independent replay.","title":"Contract and independent replay"},{"category":"section","location":"feasibility_gate/#Usage","page":"Feasibility Gate","text":"qualification = qualify_droop_replacement(source_case, candidate_case;\n    calibration_state = source_opf_state,\n    load_factors = (1.0, 0.9),\n    initial_states = [source_opf_state, source_90_state],\n    witnesses = Dict(\"source/0.9\" => [FeasibilityWitness(source_90_state)]),\n    problem_options = (angle_limits = source_angle_limits,),\n)\nwrite_droop_qualification(\"qualification.json\", qualification)\naccepted_case = require_qualified_replacement(qualification)\n# Downstream S1 analysis uses accepted_case; this line is unreachable on failure.\n\nThe calibration checks are replay-only: a local solve cannot hide a droop curve that misses the intended source point. Subsequent checks replay supplied points first, run analytical exclusion checks, and then optionally attempt a constant zero-objective local feasibility search. The search changes no droop parameters or unselected equipment. Set search_options=(solve_missing=false,) for an evidence-only replay.\n\nrequire_qualified_replacement rechecks every witness and fingerprint before returning a copy of the candidate. Do not bypass it in an acceptance workflow. Low-level case constructors and solve functions remain available for diagnostics and intentionally infeasible experiments; they are not acceptance APIs.\n\nTo qualify one individual replacement or a cumulative sequence, construct that candidate and call this same precursor at each step. Qualification of a previous candidate does not transfer to a changed set of curves.","title":"Usage"},{"category":"section","location":"feasibility_gate/#What-can-currently-be-certified-globally","page":"Feasibility Gate","text":"The built-in certificate path uses exact rational arithmetic on stored input numbers and includes the declared feasibility tolerances. It checks:\n\nActive generation capacity versus demand in every connected component. It ignores passive losses and shunt consumption, making this a necessary bound.\nReachable exact droop-Q intervals over allowed regulated-bus voltages.\nNodal reactive-power balance using these intervals, fixed/selected shunt bounds and the maximum reactive flow allowed by each terminal's thermal rating.\n\nThese are conservative outer bounds: a contradiction excludes all original feasible operating points. Certificates store their exact rational lower and upper bounds and are recomputed when reused. A solver's status is never imported as a certificate.\n\nThe default analytical path is incomplete. Optional global escalation adds a sparse outer LP with exact certificate replay, verified voltage-bound tightening, a sparse SOC relaxation, and bounded numerical global search with all droop regions. These backends are opt-in through lp_options and global_options; routine feasible-point replay does not launch them. An inconclusive backend or unsupported equipment scope remains unresolved. Solver-reported global bounds alone do not qualify as independently verified certificates.","title":"What can currently be certified globally"},{"category":"section","location":"feasibility_gate/#Current-14-bus-reproduction","page":"Feasibility Gate","text":"Run examples/s1_arpae14_feasibility_gate.jl to qualify the corrected four-percent droops, with and without deadband, at 100% and 90% demand. The script uses fixed source taps/shunts for acceptance and preserves the saved source solutions. It also replays the existing free-equipment witnesses under their own contracts and checks that they cannot pass the fixed-equipment gate. Results, frozen inputs, witnesses and attempt records are written to artifacts/s1_arpae14_feasibility_gate/.\n\nThe reproduced results are:\n\nModel 100% demand, fixed equipment 90% demand, fixed equipment 90% demand, saved free-equipment witness\nSource AC OPF, no droop replacement Feasible Feasible Not required\nFixed 4% droops with deadband Feasible Unresolved Feasible\nFixed 4% droops without deadband Feasible Unresolved Feasible\n\nBoth candidate replacements are blocked for the combined 100%/90% fixed-equipment scope. No global infeasibility certificate has been obtained for either 90% case. Free-equipment witnesses are rejected under the fixed-equipment contract.\n\nThis precursor qualifies independent S1 equilibria. Separate scenario witnesses do not establish a common preventive dispatch or shared equipment setting for S2. A coupled SCOPF qualification requires the corresponding shared/corrective control policy and movement budgets; this implementation does not claim that certificate and the example starts no SCOPF runs.","title":"Current 14-bus reproduction"},{"category":"section","location":"feasibility_gate/#API","page":"Feasibility Gate","text":"The canonical docstrings are in the API reference:\n\nFeasibilityProblem\nFeasibilityWitness\nFeasibilityResult\nDroopQualification\nfeasibility_fingerprint\nvalidate_feasibility_witness\ncheck_feasibility\nverify_feasibility_result\nwrite_feasibility_result\nread_feasibility_result\nqualify_droop_replacement\nrequire_qualified_replacement\nwrite_droop_qualification","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#M3-droop-optimization","page":"Droop Optimization","text":"M3 begins with a deliberately small, auditable problem: vary one positive volt-var slope over a bounded candidate set and solve the complete M2 security-constrained AC OPF for every setting. This establishes the reference surface that in-model optimization must reproduce.","title":"M3 droop optimization"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Contract","page":"Droop Optimization","text":"sweep_droop_slope(study, control_id, slopes):\n\npreserves the input study and changes only the selected control's slope;\nalways includes and solves the declared reference slope;\nsolves candidates outward from the reference and warm-starts from the nearest valid result;\nvalidates every candidate independently with equilibrium_report;\nrecords objective, worst voltage deviation, maximum branch loading, exact droop residual, validity, and solver termination status.\n\nThe slope uses the existing VoltVarDroop convention: per-unit voltage change per unit reactive output. Candidate and reference slopes must be positive and finite.","title":"Contract"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Example","page":"Droop Optimization","text":"sweep = sweep_droop_slope(\n    study,\n    2,\n    0.04:0.005:0.10;\n    reference_slope = 0.075,\n    smooth_epsilon = 1e-5,\n)\n\nbest = best_droop_slope(sweep)\nwrite_droop_slope_sweep(\"m3_slope_sweep.json\", sweep)\nwrite_droop_slope_sweep_plot(\"m3_slope_tradeoff.svg\", sweep)\n\nbest_droop_slope selects the valid sampled point with the smallest M2 dispatch objective. It is a parameter-search result, not a global optimality certificate. The AC model is nonconvex, and the current objective may be weakly sensitive to the droop setting, so voltage and loading diagnostics should be reviewed with the objective.\n\nHistorical figure: M3 fixed-slope security trade-off. See archived research evidence.","title":"Example"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#M2-reproduction-gate","page":"Droop Optimization","text":"When the selected slope equals the slope already stored in the study, the sweep reconstructs the same M2 problem. The test suite requires that reference point to reproduce the independently valid M2 objective. This prevents the M3 search layer from silently changing contingency policy or physical semantics.","title":"M2 reproduction gate"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Bounded-in-model-design","page":"Droop Optimization","text":"optimize_droop_parameters promotes selected physical settings to variables shared across the base case and every training contingency. Bounds omitted by the caller fix that setting at its M2 value. Available settings are:\n\npositive slope;\npositive voltage reference;\nnonnegative lower and upper deadband widths.\n\nThe two widths may differ, providing an asymmetric deadband while preserving the standard saturated VoltVarDroop family. Both widths may also be fixed at zero while slope and reference remain free; this uses the specialized affine raw response described in Solvers. Variable-width bounds that permit collapse to zero remain rejected. The active/reactive dispatch objective remains the M2 objective; no hidden design penalty is introduced.\n\ndesign = optimize_droop_parameters(\n    study,\n    2;\n    slope_bounds = (0.04, 0.10),\n    v_ref_bounds = (0.995, 1.005),\n    deadband_low_bounds = (0.005, 0.015),\n    deadband_high_bounds = (0.005, 0.015),\n    smooth_epsilon = 1e-5,\n)\n\nwith_droop_settings(study, design) reconstructs an ordinary typed study. validate_droop_design(study, design) independently replays every state against that reconstructed exact PWL curve. A design is not accepted from solver status alone.","title":"Bounded in-model design"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Solver-scope","page":"Droop Optimization","text":"Bounded parameter design is a smooth nonlinear program supported by Ipopt and MadNLP. For the M2/M3 regression fixture, MadNLP is warm-started from validated M2 states; an unseeded solve can select an infeasible local point. The two smooth solvers are required to agree on the validated objective, not on the parameter vector, because multiple settings can be nearly equivalent.\n\nCCOpt is intentionally not accepted as an optimize_droop_parameters optimizer. Once a design is reconstructed as a fixed exact curve, CCOpt can independently re-solve it through solve_scopf(optimized; encoding=:complementarity). The solver-independent validate_droop_design exact replay remains the primary acceptance gate.","title":"Solver scope"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Held-out-scenarios","page":"Droop Optimization","text":"evaluate_held_out_contingencies holds the optimized base dispatch fixed and solves contingencies whose IDs were absent from training:\n\nheld_out = evaluate_held_out_contingencies(\n    study,\n    design,\n    [Contingency(:line_33; branch_ids = [33])],\n)\nheld_out.report.valid\n\nThe returned study, result, and report retain the normal M2 data and validation contracts. write_droop_design and read_droop_design provide a versioned JSON round trip for the optimized settings and training result.","title":"Held-out scenarios"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Visual-validation","page":"Droop Optimization","text":"write_droop_design_comparison_plot overlays the reference and optimized exact PWL curves with the optimized training operating points. Held-out study and result pairs may be supplied to add their non-base scenarios in a distinct colour:\n\nwrite_droop_design_comparison_plot(\n    \"m3_droop_design_comparison.svg\",\n    study,\n    design;\n    held_out_study = held_out.study,\n    held_out_result = held_out.result,\n)\n\nThe function intentionally covers only the domain-specific curve comparison. The workflow composes it with the existing M2 scenario plots rather than introducing a separate plotting framework or dependency.\n\nHistorical figure: M3 reference and optimized droop design. See archived research evidence.\n\nThe comparison distinguishes training points from the held-out line-33 point and explicitly records that the optimized G9 control has no operating point when G9 is unavailable. The held-out result is also passed through the normal M2 residual-to-tolerance visualization; the horizontal threshold at one remains the numerical acceptance boundary.\n\nHistorical figure: M3 held-out residual validation. See archived research evidence.","title":"Visual validation"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Acceptance-gates","page":"Droop Optimization","text":"M3 requires all of the following:\n\nfixed bounds at the reference reproduce the M2 objective;\nslope-only optimization agrees with the minimum of the fixed-slope sweep;\ngeneralized bounded settings satisfy every training scenario;\nreconstructed exact curves pass independent replay;\nat least one excluded contingency passes held-out evaluation;\nthe end-to-end workflow regenerates machine-readable and visual artifacts.","title":"Acceptance gates"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Scope-limit","page":"Droop Optimization","text":"M3 optimizes the identifiable physical parameters of the standard saturated volt-var family. Reactive capability and reactive output at deadband remain fixed. Arbitrary free-knot PWL topology optimization is intentionally deferred: without a separate regularization and identifiability contract it can overfit a small contingency set while producing non-unique, hard-to-interpret curves.","title":"Scope limit"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_optimization/#Multi-controller-security-design","page":"Droop Optimization","text":"The vector DroopControl interface and iterative contingency-addition driver are documented in Droop security design. They share the ordinary SCOPF builder and retain M9 equipment policies.","title":"Multi-controller security design"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Security-constrained-AC-OPF-(M2-and-M9.1)","page":"SCOPF","text":"Historical artifacts/ paths on this page identify local campaign records, not files bundled with the release. See archive access and the current qualification index.\n\nThe SCOPF engine jointly optimizes one base dispatch and a declared set of line and generator outages. Droop settings remain fixed across scenarios. Each scenario has its own AC voltages, angles, active generation, and reactive generation. The joint formulation remains a small-network reference implementation. M10 also provides a separate conservative pre-solve filter for independent contingency assessment; it is not applied inside the joint SCOPF model.","title":"Security-constrained AC OPF (M2 and M9.1)"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#M10-contingency-catalog-and-baseline-assessment","page":"SCOPF","text":"M10's core capability is network-size agnostic. Given any validated Case and fixed base ACState, evaluate_contingency_catalog evaluates every eligible entry independently and returns its solver result, independent report, and rank. contingency_screening_scores supplies deterministic within-device priority percentiles without classifying a low-scored outage as safe:\n\ncatalog = contingency_catalog(case)\nassessments = evaluate_contingency_catalog(case, base_state;\n    catalog, participation=participation)\nscores = contingency_screening_scores(case, base_state; catalog)\ndecisions = filter_contingencies(case, base_state; catalog,\n    loading_threshold=0.8, voltage_guard=0.02)\npipeline = assess_contingency_pipeline(case, base_state; catalog,\n    audit_screened_ids=[:branch_11])\nwrite_contingency_pipeline(\"pipeline.json\", pipeline)\n\nUse normal Julia compilation for performance runs. The supervisor no longer uses --compile=min, which slows numerical model construction. Power-balance expressions for the imported FreeQ baseline contain only nonzero admittance entries; this preserves the equations while avoiding dense derivative graphs. Declared reactive-control paths retain their existing expression ordering to preserve sensitive CCOpt warm-start trajectories. Each completed baseline writes timing.json separating construction (including first-call compilation) from optimization and extraction. Compare warm runs in the same Julia process when measuring sustained throughput; a fresh process pays compilation again.\n\ncontingency_catalog(case) deterministically enumerates every available line, transformer, and generator outage. Each candidate records its device ID and a structural disposition. Connected outages and generator outages are eligible; a line or transformer outage that islands the network is retained as excluded from the original single-component evaluator with reason islanding_unsupported. It can instead be passed to evaluate_islanding_contingency. That path retains the original-reference component unless another component has strictly more buses. If the larger component is selected, the bus of its available generator with greatest p_max becomes the reference (generator ID breaks ties). Only the retained component is balanced; discarded buses, load, and generation capacity are reported and are never classified as served or safe.\n\nfilter_contingencies is a conservative routing layer. Generator outages go to exact AC evaluation. Structurally islanding line and transformer outages go to the island-aware evaluator. Connected network outages use a DC LODF estimate and are screened only when predicted loading is below the declared threshold and the base voltage is outside the guard band. Singular or uncertain calculations, near-limit base voltages, and predicted thermal risk all fall back to exact AC evaluation. Screening is a workload decision, not an AC security certificate; thresholds require held-out reliability qualification before operational use.\n\nassess_contingency_pipeline executes those routes and provides the integrated checks around them. It validates the base state, requires exactly one decision for every catalog entry, checks decision identity against the catalog, retains per-contingency evaluation errors, and independently validates every executed AC result. Generator outages always use exact fixed-base SCOPF assessment; islanding line/transformer outages use retained-component AC assessment; screened connected network outages remain explicitly screened_not_evaluated.\n\nThe optional audit_screened_ids list selects deterministic screened cases for an exact AC cross-check. An unresolved audited case becomes screening_audit_failure, moves to the top of the combined ranking, and makes the pipeline completeness check fail. screening_enabled=false converts every otherwise screened connected outage into an exact baseline run. The result reports routing_complete separately from full_ac_coverage and all_outcomes_accepted; therefore a successfully routed filtered run cannot be mistaken for complete AC security assessment. The result contains aggregate network, line, transformer, and generator rankings plus a combined operational ranking. The combined ordering uses explicit tiers—screening-audit failure, evaluation error, invalid result, island load loss, physical-only result, accepted exact result, then unaudited screened entry—before severity and stable ID tie-breaks. The aggregate network ranking is retained for compatibility, while line and transformer rankings are separate families.\n\nThe diagnostic baseline runner evaluates a numbered batch against one independently validated, fixed base OPF state:\n\nUse the supplied POSIX supervisor for a bounded batch. It records its own completion or timeout in supervisor.md and retains run.log even if the child never reaches Ipopt. An operating-system CPU-limit signal is explicitly recorded as cpu_limited_unresolved:\n\nThe integrated runner writes campaign.md and campaign.json directly. The report command recognizes these files; for historical campaigns it still generates the legacy metric-specific coverage and timing report:\n\nOnly for historical-format, complete exact enumerations, the old inexpensive base-state loading/outage-size heuristic can be benchmarked with:\n\nThe benchmark reports unresolved-case recall at fixed evaluation budgets and the smallest retrospective budget that captures every unresolved exact outcome. It does not activate filtering: thresholds selected and evaluated on the same campaign cannot establish out-of-sample safety. rank_contingencies(catalog, report) ranks only outages represented in the report; invalid or physical-only outcomes remain visible ahead of accepted ones, and unlisted outages are never presumed safe. This is a fixed-base security assessment, not a security-constrained redispatch solve. Complete all batches before comparing ranking or filtering methods to the enumeration baseline. The production routing API uses LODF only after this exact baseline was established; it is not silently enabled in the historical IEEE campaigns. The shared-droop scenario-addition driver is documented in Droop security design; parallel execution remains future work.","title":"M10 contingency catalog and baseline assessment"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Opt-in-droop-response-guard","page":"SCOPF","text":"droop_response_guard=true adds individual generator/controller Q headroom, bounded AC/droop-response prediction and controller-regime transition guards. It only changes screened branches to exact evaluation; it never changes exact or island-aware routes into screens. Unsupported or uncertain predictions fall back to exact AC. Pass the same participation weights used by subsequent exact assessment. The flag is supported by filtering, the integrated pipeline, qualification and resumable campaigns, and is included in campaign fingerprints. The default is false. See P2-02 qualification for the frozen protocol and current evidence; saved-case improvement is not held-out qualification or a security certificate.","title":"Opt-in droop-response guard"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Generic-resumable-campaigns-and-filtering-qualification","page":"SCOPF","text":"run_contingency_campaign(directory, case, base_state) supports arbitrary networks and the integrated screened, exact, audited and island-aware routes. Set screening_enabled=false for the exhaustive AC reference. Its returned result is a ContingencyPipelineResult; resumed_count, evaluated_count and fingerprint describe reuse. Completion refers to the supplied catalog; a subset does not imply complete network N-1 coverage.\n\nThe atomic JSON checkpoints bind the complete case/catalog, saved base, participation, screening thresholds, audit IDs, numerical settings, code, Julia version and dependency files. A changed contract is rejected, not silently reused. Corrupt/incomplete checkpoints and evaluation errors are rerun. Saved states are revalidated and reranked; saved acceptance flags are not trusted. Invalid completed solves can be resumed as unresolved evidence. To retry with different settings, choose a new directory. The directory lock prevents concurrent writers; after a forcibly killed process, verify no writer is active before removing its stale .campaign-lock directory. Checkpoints provide process-interruption recovery, not a power-loss durability guarantee.\n\nvalidate_contingency_filter(case, base_state) compares filter decisions with exhaustive AC routes on identical inputs, separately for lines, transformers and generators. It reports screened unresolved outcomes, screened physical failures, and counts found at each evaluation budget. Solver errors/rejections are unresolved, not proven infeasibility. Recall is undefined if there are no unresolved cases. Evaluation order is mandatory routes first, then descending predicted loading with stable ID ties; post-evaluation severity ranking remains a separate output. LODF factorization is reused across candidate transactions; nonfinite predictions and unsupported numerical conditions fall back to AC.\n\nThe default filter now additionally uses reactive_guard=0.1 and ac_risk_guard=true. The first compares aggregate upward/downward generator Q headroom with available Q range. The second escalates predicted apparent-flow loading and large impedance-based voltage-drop changes after redistribution. Neither is an AC solution or a mathematically certified bound. They only escalate: they cannot change an existing exact route into a screen. Generator outages remain exact. Both settings are included in campaign fingerprints; changed settings require a new campaign directory.\n\nRun the broader small-network qualification and timing experiment sequentially:\n\njulia --project=. examples/scopf_filter_qualification.jl artifacts/scopf_filter_qualification/baseline baseline both\njulia --project=. examples/scopf_filter_qualification.jl artifacts/scopf_filter_qualification/enhanced enhanced both\njulia --project=. examples/scopf_filter_qualification.jl artifacts/scopf_filter_qualification summary\n\nThe predeclared discovery partition contains twelve 3/5-bus cases; held-out contains twelve 4/6-bus cases. Ring/chord topology, demand, reactive reserve, generation location and transformer taps vary. Legacy thresholds are frozen at 0.8 loading and 0.02 pu voltage margin; its additional guards are disabled. Enhanced settings were frozen after discovery and before held-out evaluation. Per-family unresolved recall is compared with exhaustive AC assessment. Reports retain failures and rejected bases, rather than silently omitting them.\n\nThree paired timing repetitions alternate execution order. The first is excluded from warmed results. Phase timing separates filter, preparation/model building, optimization/extraction and independent validation/ranking; report serialization is timed separately and excludes disk I/O and plotting. First-comparison wall and runtime-provided compilation time are retained separately; they exclude process startup, package precompilation/loading and base construction. Phase times and compile counters are not claimed to be an exact disjoint accounting. The evidence index is artifacts/scopf_filter_qualification/evaluation.md.\n\nThe first frozen comparison covered 168 outages over those 24 inputs. Legacy screening missed two unresolved branch outages in the discovery reactive-stress case; the enhanced guards escalated both. Both policies had zero observed misses on 90 held-out outages. The enhanced policy screened 44 held-out branch outages (legacy: 66), so its extra caution has a measurable workload cost. Warm screened/exhaustive speedup for the enhanced policy was 1.22× on discovery and 1.31× on held-out, based on summed per-input medians, not a large-system forecast. First-comparison JIT dominated approximately nine seconds of cold execution. All held-out branch outages passed the exhaustive reference; held-out branch failure recall is therefore undefined, not 100%. The observed unresolved held-out cases were generator outages, which remain exact under both policies. After the enhancement, the full unit suite passed 2,626/2,626 tests in 3m35.0s; the documentation build passed with its API-page size warning only. Coverage and phase-timing SVGs were rendered and visually inspected.","title":"Generic resumable campaigns and filtering qualification"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Response-contract","page":"SCOPF","text":"All active/reactive powers and redispatch limits are per-unit on case.base_power; voltage magnitudes are per-unit and angles are radians. Positive P/Q means network injection. The reference bus fixes only its angle; it does not supply unbounded balancing power. All available generators retain their capability bounds.\n\nPreventive mode uses a base schedule and limit-aware automatic active response:\n\nP_gc=operatornameclip(P_g0+alpha_gclambda_c L_gc U_gc)\n\nEffective bounds intersect generator and attached droop active capability with any declared base-to-outage redispatch_limits. A zero-weight unit holds its base output. Unavailable generators supply zero and are excluded. Zero-range units are excluded from normalized response weights. As the common signal grows, a participant at a bound stays there and unsaturated participants continue responding. If total reserve is inadequate, the model does not invent balancing power or relax operating bounds.\n\nStudy and assessment APIs default to default_participation(case): nonzero effective active-capability-range weights. Supply actual governor/AGC weights when known; the default is an explicit approximation. An explicit empty dictionary selects no participants and is rejected when an outage requires response. The surviving weights are normalized. Weights do not encode response speed.\n\nThe retained balancing_power result field contains the common request signal lambda, in pu. After saturation it is not the total delivered adjustment; calculate that from surviving generators' actual base-to-outage changes. The smooth reactive encoding uses an exact piecewise active projection (min/max) with kinks at saturation. The complementarity encoding uses projection KKT conditions with nonnegative bound slacks and multipliers. Both represent the same exact bounded active-response law. Coupled preventive CCOpt models default both the solver and proportional-update relaxation floors to 1e-14; supplied optimizer attributes can override these numerical defaults. This tightens the internal relaxation without changing physical acceptance tolerances. Projection slacks enforce equipment bounds without duplicate active variable-bound rows. A sole movable participant uses an equivalent canonical request-equals-delivery signal. It is not a smooth governor dynamic model; local convergence and saturation-boundary behavior require validation.\n\nCurrent study/result JSON carries active_response=\"limit_aware_v1\" (study v10, result v5). Historical coupled preventive files without this marker are rejected, rather than silently reinterpreted. Replay them with their original source. Campaign fingerprints include the response contract; use a fresh output directory for the new policy. Historical filtering thresholds and performance outcomes require requalification before use as evidence for this policy.\n\nCorrective mode replaces this automatic-response equation with:\n\n-r_g le P_gc-P_g0le r_g\n\nredispatch_limits[g] supplies the finite, nonnegative symmetric bound r_g. An omitted generator has zero permitted redispatch. This represents controlled post-contingency redispatch, without a time or ramp-rate model. Participation weights are rejected in this mode to avoid an ambiguous policy.\n\nIn both modes:\n\nUnavailable generators inject zero P/Q and their droop equation is omitted.\nUnavailable branches contribute neither admittance nor flow.\nAvailable controlled generators obey their fixed Q(V) relation at the declared regulated bus in each scenario. No voltage setpoint is optimized.\nGenerators without a droop attachment retain M1's independently adjustable Q within capability limits. Attach controls to every unit when autonomous volt-var equilibrium is the intended experiment.\nLoads, branch ratings, and voltage limits are unchanged across scenarios.\nDisconnected base networks are rejected by the joint SCOPF formulation. Islanding outages use the separate retained-component assessment described above.\nReactive capability is M1's constant Q interval with a permitted P interval; a curved P-dependent capability boundary remains outside this implementation.","title":"Response contract"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Equipment-and-reactive-control-timing-(M9.1)","page":"SCOPF","text":"Study may select continuous transformer ratios and simple-bank susceptances with the same TapControl and ShuntControl policies used by base-case design. Timing is explicit:\n\npolicy = SCOPFControlPolicy(\n    taps = :corrective,       # default: scenario-specific within bounds\n    shunts = :corrective,     # default: scenario-specific within bounds\n    reactive = :automatic,    # the only supported reactive timing\n    # Optional M9.2 maps: listed devices receive ± movement budgets;\n    # selected but unlisted corrective devices are frozen.\n    tap_movement_limits = nothing,\n    shunt_movement_limits = nothing,\n)\n\nstudy = Study(case;\n    contingencies,\n    participation,\n    reactive_assignments,\n    tap_controls = [TapControl(11; lower=0.95, upper=1.05)],\n    shunt_controls = [ShuntControl(201)],\n    control_policy = policy,\n)\n\nCorrective equipment has an independent bounded setting in each scenario and is the default for selected tap and shunt controls. Preventive equipment has one setting shared by the base case and every scenario in which the device remains available; request it explicitly. These options may be selected separately for taps and shunts. A controlled transformer that is itself outaged has no contingency setting or coupling equation. This is availability, not corrective movement.\n\nReactive modes are automatic: the assignment, droop curve, AVR voltage target, FixedQ schedule, and regulated location are shared across scenarios, while Q, voltage, AVR limit regime, and the set of surviving common-location regulators respond to scenario physics. Scenario-specific voltage targets or droop parameters are not introduced. A branch-terminal regulator is disabled when its monitoring branch is outaged, as in the fixed-equipment SCOPF path.\n\nTap ratios and shunt susceptances remain continuous relaxations. Corrective timing asserts that movement is allowed in the modeled post-contingency stage; it does not supply a response time, switching count, discrete recovery, or dynamic feasibility claim. The caller is responsible for making that timing credible for the equipment represented.\n\nM9.2 movement maps refine corrective timing. nothing retains M9.1 behavior: every selected corrective device may choose any setting inside its absolute control bounds. A supplied dictionary declares the allowed-action set. Listed devices satisfy abs(setting[c]-setting[base]) <= limit; selected devices omitted from the dictionary are frozen at their base setting. Limits are finite, nonnegative, and independently replayed by the validator.\n\nLong assessments can solve the two M9.3 stages in separate processes and then call assemble_scopf_response_stages(immediate_study, settled_study, immediate, settled). This performs the same independent validation and metric assembly as assess_scopf_response_stages without rerunning either solve. The S2 public runner uses this path so an external wall timeout cannot erase a completed stage.\n\nThe process-isolated S2 example also supports a separate recovery lane that seeds a fixed-equipment solve from a direct SCOPF witness across every scenario. It is diagnostic evidence only: its outcome must not overwrite the declared FreeQ-base fixed-seed qualification lane. A physically replay-valid solution with a rejected solver status may be used as an initialization witness, but is still rejected for solver-acceptance reporting.\n\nWhen the direct equipment-aware result is already independently valid, M9.3 may also be anchored directly to its saved base state and equipment settings. This is distinct from claiming success for a nominal-equipment staging lane. Reports must identify the anchor lineage and keep immediate, settled, physical-replay, and solver-acceptance outcomes separate.\n\nThe objective is the existing M1 base schedule-deviation objective:\n\nmin sum_g (P_g0-P_g^rm initial)^2\n       + 10^-3(Q_g0-Q_g^rm initial)^2\n\nContingency states impose feasibility constraints without an additional cost. Consequently, corrective dispatch can be nonunique. This is not an economic SCOPF cost model. With no contingencies, the problem reproduces M1.","title":"Equipment and reactive-control timing (M9.1)"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Solve-and-evaluate","page":"SCOPF","text":"study = Study(case;\n    contingencies = [\n        Contingency(:line_22; branch_ids = [22]),\n        Contingency(:generator_9; generator_ids = [9]),\n    ],\n    participation = default_participation(case),\n)\nresult = solve(study)\nreport = equilibrium_report(study, result)\nprintln(markdown_report(report))\n\n# Assess an existing base equilibrium without changing its dispatch or voltage.\nevaluation = evaluate_contingencies(study, result.states[:base])\n\n# Alternatively, allow bounded corrective redispatch.\ncorrective = Study(case; contingencies = study.contingencies,\n    mode = :corrective, redispatch_limits = Dict(7 => 0.8, 9 => 0.8))\ncorrective_result = solve_scopf(corrective)\n\nIDs refer to device IDs, not array positions. State vectors retain original case order, including zero entries for outaged generators. Results use :base and the contingency IDs as dictionary keys. No state is silently treated as feasible: a failed solve may return an infeasible iterate, or nothing if none is available. During fixed-base evaluation, the supplied base is independently validated but is not rebuilt as a redundant set of fixed equations in the NLP.\n\nThe default solver is Ipopt. optimizer_factory=MadNLP.Optimizer uses the same smooth formulation. encoding=:complementarity selects CCOpt and the exact PWL formulation; omit optimizer_factory for this encoding. Solver options are passed through optimizer_attributes.\n\nsolve_scopf_continuation(study; smooth_epsilons=[1e-2,1e-3,1e-4]) returns the sequence of results, warm-starting all scenario states. It stops after solver failure or a missing state. Independently validate the final result. For other initial points, pass a dictionary through initial_states to solve_scopf.\n\nEquipment variables use each control's initial value. For difficult joint models, first solve the fixed-equipment SCOPF and pass its scenario states as a staged start; then seed a corrective run's control initials from the validated preventive settings. A solver's infeasible termination is retained and is not treated as a physical infeasibility certificate.\n\nresult.tap_settings and result.shunt_settings are scenario-indexed maps of the selected controls only. Fixed-base evaluation accepts an ACState; studies with selected equipment must also pass the exact saved base maps through fixed_base_tap_settings and fixed_base_shunt_settings. Missing or extra settings are rejected rather than guessed.\n\nM9.3 pairs two assessments around that same saved base:\n\nassessment = assess_scopf_response_stages(\n    immediate_preventive_study,\n    settled_corrective_study,\n    validated_base_result,\n)\nwrite_scopf_stage_assessment(\"stages.json\", assessment)\nprintln(markdown_report(assessment))\n\nThe immediate study must freeze taps and shunts. The settled study may apply M9.2 budgets. The report compares voltage, thermal and generator-Q margins plus equipment movement. It represents two steady-state equilibria, not elapsed time, dynamics, switching sequences, or transient stability.","title":"Solve and evaluate"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Independent-acceptance-checks","page":"SCOPF","text":"equilibrium_report(study, result) rebuilds the outage cases and checks AC balance, both ends of branch flows, generator and voltage limits, exact droop replay, disabled generators, reference angles, active-response coupling, and equipment timing. For every scenario it reconstructs a physical network from the reported tap and shunt settings before calculating residuals. Missing, out-of-bounds, nonfinite, or unexpectedly present settings are rejected; for preventive controls, deviation from the base setting is reported separately as equipment_coupling_violation. The report records tolerances and scenario-specific failures.\n\nphysical_valid requires exact-curve equilibrium and all physical/coupling checks.\nencoded_physical_valid checks the numerical droop encoding in place of exact replay.\nvalid additionally requires a successful solver termination. For a fixed-base evaluation with no contingencies there is no optimization; status is :NOT_RUN and independent feasibility alone determines validity.\n\nThe report lists exact and encoded droop residuals separately, along with their smoothing gap. Default power/limit/coupling tolerances are 1e-6, exact and encoded droop tolerance is 1e-5, and unavailable-generator tolerance is 1e-8. Tolerances are explicit keyword arguments. These checks certify only the reported equilibrium to those tolerances, not global optimality, uniqueness, transient stability, or frequency response.","title":"Independent acceptance checks"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Reproducibility","page":"SCOPF","text":"Run from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=. examples/m2_workflow.jl /tmp/droopopf-m2\n\nThe example writes a study, result, and validation report as JSON, plus a Markdown report. It reloads the JSON files, independently validates the restored result, and deliberately corrupts an outage result to demonstrate rejection.\n\nThe M9.1 workflow stages a preventive continuous tap/shunt SCOPF from a fixed-equipment witness, then solves a separately declared corrective-timing study. Both are independently validated and persisted:\n\njulia --project=. examples/m9_1_equipment_scopf.jl /tmp/droopopf-m9-1\n\nwrite_study(\"study.json\", study)\nwrite_scopf_result(\"result.json\", result)\nwrite_scopf_report(\"report.json\", report)\nrestored_study = read_study(\"study.json\")\nrestored_result = read_scopf_result(\"result.json\")\nrestored_report = equilibrium_report(restored_study, restored_result)","title":"Reproducibility"},{"category":"section","location":"scopf/#Visual-validation","page":"SCOPF","text":"The end-to-end workflow writes nine self-contained SVG figures. Five detailed operating plots are retained:\n\nm2_droop_operating_points.svg overlays base and contingency operating points on the exact volt-var curves. Marker shape identifies the scenario; curve colour identifies the control.\nm2_bus_voltages.svg compares each bus across scenarios and shows its allowable voltage interval behind every operating point.\nm2_branch_loading.svg reports apparent-power loading as a percentage of rating, draws the 100% threshold, and marks outaged branches with a cross.\nm2_generator_dispatch.svg compares active and reactive generation and marks outaged generators.\nm2_validation_residuals.svg plots AC-balance, exact-droop, and response residuals divided by their acceptance tolerances. The horizontal threshold is one; bars below it pass.\n\nFour cross-scenario evaluation plots connect declared inputs to independently checked outputs:\n\nscopf_declared_inputs.svg lists each outage, active-power response policy, and preventive/corrective tap and shunt timing;\nscopf_operating_margins.svg shows voltage, thermal, generator-P, and generator-Q margins, with near-limit and violated cells highlighted;\nscopf_response_deltas.svg compares maximum voltage, angle, active-power, and reactive-power changes from the saved base state on a logarithmic scale;\nscopf_validation_matrix.svg maps every scenario to independent state, balance, droop/AVR, voltage, thermal, capability, availability, response, and equipment checks. It is intentionally writable for invalid results so negative controls and failed solves can be investigated visually.\n\nGenerate the complete bundle with:\n\npaths = write_scopf_validation_plots(\"m2-validation\", study, result)\n\nIndividual writers are available when only one view is needed: write_scopf_droop_plot, write_scopf_voltage_plot, write_scopf_branch_loading_plot, write_scopf_dispatch_plot, and write_scopf_residual_plot. A missing scenario state or a result whose scenario set differs from the study is rejected instead of producing a partial figure. The graphics explain a validated result; the independent numerical report remains the acceptance authority.\n\nRun julia --project=. examples/scopf_edge_case_evaluation.jl OUTPUT to produce matched bundles for an accepted base/line/generator study and a deliberately invalid negative controls with nonzero reactive output on the outaged generator, missing state, excessive voltage, and altered active generation. These corruptions test validation sensitivity; they are not feasible stressed operating points. The example also solves changed-input cases with tight thermal ratings and insufficient post-generator-outage capacity, retaining rejected results for review. Each directory includes evaluation.md, which embeds all nine figures and the authoritative numerical validation report in one reviewable input/output index. The report separates solver acceptance, exact physical validity, and encoded physical validity, states assumptions and tolerances, and saves complete evaluation-inputs.json, evaluation-outputs.json, and evaluation-validation.json records. Loading plots reconstruct solved taps and shunts. Missing or malformed states produce NOT EVALUATED cells and labelled gaps rather than passes. Available scenarios remain visible; voltage trajectories never bridge a missing scenario. Without a base, voltage/loading/dispatch plots still show available outages, but base-relative deltas are unavailable. Legacy droop plots are N/A for free-Q/explicit-assignment studies. Independent numeric checks remain the authority, including for solver-rejected states that can still be inspected. PASS is limited to the declared scenarios and checks, not dynamic security or global optimality. Optional context text labels the provenance of each bundle.\n\nThe same example now produces a single root evaluation.md linking all evidence: accepted/corrupted three-bus states; two-bus thermal, voltage, reactive-capacity and non-unity-transformer fixtures; exhaustive filtering comparisons and budget plots; and a loaded five-bus island campaign. Interior bases are chosen with AVR targets (1.009 pu for the tight-voltage fixture, 1.0 otherwise), while the declared post-outage reactive response is free Q within limits. A deliberately disabled-voltage-guard run tests the benchmark's ability to identify false screens. Retained island validity is reported beside excluded demand and reference choice; it never implies that discarded islands were balanced. This is small-network qualification, not operational deployment certification. Large public networks remain S1/S2 reliability/robustness inputs only, never unit-test fixtures.\n\nQualification snapshot (2026-10-03): the five guarded two-bus fixtures covered 20 connected outages, including six unresolved outcomes. Six branch outages were screened with zero observed misses. Disabling the voltage guard on the tight-voltage fixture produced two false screens (one line and one transformer), both detected by the exhaustive comparison. All four loaded island branch outages validated on their retained components while explicitly reporting excluded demand (0.03 or 0.12 pu). Campaign replay reused all seven island-case checkpoints without executing another route. The full unit suite passed 2,574/2,574 tests in 3m50.9s; the documentation build passed with its API-page size warning only. SVGs were rendered and representative accepted, missing, rejected, ranking and island figures visually inspected. The generated evidence index is artifacts/scopf_validation/evaluation.md; regenerate it using the example above. These are fixture-level observations, not held-out certification.\n\nJSON documents use a document-kind tag. Study schema v10 and result schema v5 persist optional reactive-control assignments, selected tap/shunt policies, the timing/movement policy, and scenario equipment settings, with an explicit limit-aware active-response marker. Historical coupled preventive documents without the marker require their original source for replay. Supported uncoupled and corrective documents retain the existing equipment/attachment migrations. Selected equipment policies default to corrective timing; unselected taps and shunts remain supplied fixed data. Study files contain all case data, contingencies, and response policy. Result files contain scenario states, active-response request signals, equipment settings, solver/status, encoding, and smoothing widths. Nonfinite diagnostics are written as JSON null; missing states remain null. Report files are diagnostic exports; reports are regenerated from study/results, not trusted as proof of validity. Save the Julia project/manifest alongside an experiment when exact dependency versions matter.","title":"Visual validation"},{"category":"section","location":"release_scope/#Release-scope-—-v0.10.0-rc1","page":"Release scope","text":"This is a software prerelease, not closure of all research milestones.\n\nArea Released implementation Qualification boundary\nS1 Base AC OPF and reactive/equipment design Declared sampled 14/500-bus studies; original data assumptions apply\nS2 Shared security design and scenario assessment Declared 14-bus scope closed; original 500-bus research remains paused\nP1/P2 Sparse models, validation, initialization and bounded scenario workflows No general safe predictive skipping or full-catalogue resource/loss optimality claim\nP3 multi-period Shared curves, dispatch ramps, outage recourse and AVR schedules Deterministic sampled steady states, no storage or dynamic trajectories\nP3 parser RAW 33/34/36 and saved-state replay Strict AC subset; native v36 export qualification pending\nMixed controls AVR and generic volt-var with apparent-power circles Detailed synchronous-machine and GFL/GFM dynamics deferred\n\nThe latest selected mixed 500-bus design uses 15 AVR and 36 droop units, assumed S=Pmax/0.95, five hourly demand factors 90/100/105/100/90%, full load, normal bounds, limit-aware active response and shared designed curves. All 389 connected outages pass independent audits. Corrective controls remain continuous: 131 taps, supplied +/-5% absolute envelope and <=0.025 supplied-ratio movement; 11 banks within legal envelopes and <=two-section movement. The assumed 120-second settled stage is not a verified device delay. AVR targets are preventive within each period and move <=0.01 pu/hour. No physical limits were relaxed for the final recovery. The objective was feasibility, not a certified minimum-loss or maximum-reserve solution.\n\nDeferred: general island balancing, dynamic/small-signal validation, discrete switching and verified device timing, current-limited GFL/GFM models, storage, renewable availability, full coupled resource optimization and broad screening qualification. The earlier full model used roughly 17 GiB RAM; separating saved research files does not change that numerical memory requirement.\n\nSee research evidence for exact historical contracts and manifests.\n\nSee the milestone index and selected validation figures and reports.","title":"Release scope — v0.10.0-rc1"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Development","page":"Development","text":"","title":"Development"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Local-checks","page":"Development","text":"Run the package tests from the repository root:\n\nJULIA_NUM_THREADS=2 OPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 julia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.test()'\n\nBuild the documentation locally:\n\njulia --project=docs -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path = \".\"); Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=docs docs/make.jl\n\nThe generated site is in docs/build/. Two Julia threads exercise the bounded contingency worker tests; serial-only runs skip that concurrency branch.","title":"Local checks"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Continuous-integration-and-deployment","page":"Development","text":".github/workflows/CI.yml runs the package tests on Julia 1.10 and 1.12. .github/workflows/Documentation.yml builds the docs for pull requests and deploys the main documentation to GitHub Pages through Documenter.jl.\n\nThe deployment workflow requires GitHub Pages to be enabled for the repository. Documenter uses the repository GITHUB_TOKEN, so no deploy key is needed for this same-repository deployment.","title":"Continuous integration and deployment"},{"category":"section","location":"development/#Milestone-and-qualification-records","page":"Development","text":"Use the milestone index for navigation and the release scope for current qualification boundaries. M1–M4 are historical implementation milestones; their original small-case scale-up decision is not the current status. Sparse models, scenario addition, parallel assessment and 14/500-bus studies now have bounded qualification. General safe predictive skipping and full coupled resource/loss optimization remain open. Original S2 remains paused; later P2 evidence is a separate campaign.\n\nSee the validation references for selected numerical results and figures, and research evidence for archived reports. Package tests check software regressions; they do not rerun the large campaigns.","title":"Milestone and qualification records"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_security/#Droop-design-with-contingency-addition","page":"Droop Security","text":"Historical artifacts/ paths on this page identify local campaign records, not files bundled with the release. See archive access and the current qualification index.","title":"Droop design with contingency addition"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_security/#Physical-and-acceptance-contract","page":"Droop Security","text":"The design variables are selected controllers' positive slopes (pu voltage per pu reactive power), voltage references (pu), and nonnegative lower/upper deadband widths (pu). One setting vector is shared by the base and every training outage. Reactive capability and deadband output remain supplied data. The existing saturated Volt–VAr law, AC balance equations, operating limits and base schedule-deviation objective are unchanged. Each scenario has independent AC states; an outaged generator has zero P/Q and no active controller equation. Active response follows the Study participation/redispatch policy. Selected taps and shunts retain their preventive/corrective bounds and movement budgets.\n\nThe smooth design NLP uses the existing SCOPF builder. Independent validation reconstructs exact physical curves and checks the design bounds, AC physics, outage availability and inter-scenario coupling. Fixed design bounds must reproduce fixed-curve SCOPF; no contingencies must reproduce base-case design. Multiple local optima may exist: objective agreement is an experimental check, not a global-optimality assertion.\n\nContingency addition starts from a declared subset, solves a shared design, recomputes screening at the new base/settings, assesses required outages, and adds unresolved omitted outages. Solver rejection is unresolved evidence, not proven infeasibility. Screening alone never permits successful termination: every eligible outage receives a full AC audit at the final design. Islanding entries remain explicit exclusions from the joint design and prevent a full network N-1 claim. Iteration limits, failed masters and unresolved training outages remain unsuccessful outcomes.\n\nFor controller j, with slope mj, reference vj and half-widths dj^- and dj^+, the independently replayed response is\n\nQ_jc=operatornameclipleft(Q_j^0+\nfracv_j-d_j^- - V_jc_+m_j-\nfracV_jc-v_j-d_j^+_+m_j Q_j^minQ_j^maxright)\n\nAll design parameters are shared over c. The base objective remains\n\nmin sum_g left(P_g0-P_g^mathrminitial)^2\n+10^-3(Q_g0-Q_g^mathrminitial)^2right\n\nFor preventive active response, surviving generators obey P[g,c] = clip(P[g,0] + alpha[g,c]*signal[c], L[g,c], U[g,c]), with capability-aware weights renormalised over survivors and saturation allowing remaining participants to take over. Zero-weight units hold base output. Corrective mode instead enforces the declared redispatch bounds. The final audit holds the complete base AC state and base equipment settings fixed; it never reoptimises a different base separately for each outage.","title":"Physical and acceptance contract"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_security/#API-and-reproducible-runs","page":"Droop Security","text":"catalog = contingency_catalog(case)\nbase_study = Study(case; participation=participation)\ncontrols = [DroopControl(1; slope_bounds=(0.04,0.08)),\n            DroopControl(2; slope_bounds=(0.04,0.10))]\nresult = optimize_droop_security(base_study, controls;\n    catalog, max_rounds=5, batch_size=1,\n    initial_states=Dict(:base=>validated_base),\n    solver_kwargs=(;smooth_epsilon=1e-5,\n        optimizer_attributes=Dict(\"max_iter\"=>1000,\"max_cpu_time\"=>30.0)))\nwrite_droop_security(\"security.json\", result)\n\nUse optimize_droop_parameters(study, controls) for the corresponding full training-set design. validate_droop_design(study, design) returns valid, policy_valid, and the ordinary independent report. The scalar M3 interface remains available and now preserves equipment policy during reconstruction and held-out evaluation. Attached legacy droops and explicit reactive assignments cannot be combined in this design interface; unsupported combinations reject rather than silently dropping a controller policy.\n\nDroopSecurityResult.eligible_accepted covers the supplied eligible catalog. full_n1_accepted additionally requires the complete network catalog and no excluded islands. A successful result contains a final exhaustive audit; a failed master contains its validation outcome and never receives an inferred security pass. on_iteration can checkpoint the entire round after assessment. Independent assessment errors are retained, and added IDs use deterministic error/violation ordering. The algorithm adds scenario constraints, not linear cuts or a decomposition with convergence/global-optimality guarantees.\n\nsparse_balance=true opts smooth OPF/SCOPF into omitting exact-zero admittance terms even with droop controls. It changes expression sparsity, not equations. Legacy controlled-case defaults remain unchanged because solver trajectories can be sensitive to numerical representation. The public runner declares this option and bounded solver settings in its protocol. Time limits apply during optimization, not package loading or model construction.\n\nThe plotting runner requires Matplotlib. Public exhaustive campaigns use the existing input-fingerprinted checkpoints; a changed case, source, policy or solver budget requires a new directory. The subsequent design trace is retained round-by-round but is not automatically resumable. Fresh design runs must use fresh evidence directories if prior results are to be preserved.\n\nTo assess only branch contingencies (lines and transformers), retain the original runs and use separate directories:\n\nThe branch-only catalog retains islanding branch outages in the exhaustive campaign and excludes generator outages entirely. The joint design still handles only connected outages. A successful eligible branch audit therefore does not imply full-network N-1 acceptance. retained/ saves the valid master with the largest training set, then most independently accepted outages, then lowest objective. Its acceptance manifest lists the exact coverage; a later rejected master never replaces this candidate with an invalid design. optimized-study.json and master-result.json use the public read_study and read_scopf_result interfaces. The replay example verifies their round trip, independently revalidates every saved accepted outage, and checks that all witnesses use the identical retained base state. The comparison report also checks that the original evidence files have not changed against the saved verification/pre-branch-preservation.json manifest (run it from the repository root). The optional --prior replay exports accepted branch witnesses from an earlier fixed-base campaign separately. It verifies matching study/base inputs and revalidates the saved states; it does not re-solve them or count them as new successful attempts.","title":"API and reproducible runs"},{"category":"section","location":"droop_security/#Initial-measured-evidence","page":"Droop Security","text":"The three-bus reference accepted all five N-1 outages. Two shared slope variables converged in two design rounds: one line outage was added and all five outages were independently audited. Full enumeration and iterative objectives differed by approximately 2.1e-12. Cold-start rejection is retained separately from the declared proportional-region base initialization.\n\nThe unchanged filter was then evaluated on 168 actual-droop outages across 24 inputs. It missed five unresolved discovery outages and seven held-out outages (six lines and one transformer); all twelve missed states failed physical replay. This does not qualify screening as a security certificate. Held-out accepted states covered deadband, proportional response and Q-max saturation; no accepted Q-min saturation point occurred in this partition. The final exhaustive audit is therefore a required algorithmic acceptance gate.\n\nSee artifacts/droop_security/reference/report.md and artifacts/droop_security/filter/report.md for saved inputs, numerical results, iteration figures, filter coverage, ranking-budget curves and control regimes.\n\nAt half of each held-out input's evaluation budget, the diagnostic ranking found 7/15 unresolved line outages and 11/21 generator outages; it has not yet demonstrated useful prioritisation. See artifacts/droop_security/report.md for the complete evidence and remaining qualification gates.","title":"Initial measured evidence"},{"category":"section","location":"psse/#PSS/E-RAW-data-parser","page":"PSS/E","text":"Historical artifacts/ paths on this page identify local campaign records, not files bundled with the release. See archive access and the current qualification index.\n\nparse_psse_raw reads full-case bus/branch RAW revisions 33, 34 and 36. import_psse_case converts the supported electrical subset into a DroopOPF Case, with source identities, supplied operating points and diagnostics. load_psse_case returns just the converted case.\n\nraw = parse_psse_raw(\"network.raw\")\nraw.revision\nraw.sections[:generator][1].fields\n\n# Strict conversion: zero/omitted ratings require an explicit decision.\nimported = import_psse_case(raw; unrated_limit=10.0) # pu, only if intended\ncase = imported.case\nsupplied_state = imported.initial_state\nidentities = imported.source_ids\nmessages = imported.diagnostics\n\nThere are no added package dependencies or PSS/E installation requirements. This parser does not read binary .sav, .dyr, .seq or JSON .rawx files. IC=1 change-case files are rejected because they need an existing network. No optimization is launched by parsing or importing.","title":"PSS/E RAW data parser"},{"category":"section","location":"psse/#Version-handling","page":"PSS/E","text":"Feature v33 v34 v36\nBus fields and supplied Vm/Va Parsed Parsed Parsed\nLoad fields Original layout Distributed-generation fields Text load type and name\nFixed shunts Original layout Same layout Additional name\nGenerator fields Original layout NREG at tail NREG before MBASE, BASLOD, droop-controller name and machine name\nBranch ratings Three ratings Name and twelve ratings Twelve ratings plus bypass flag\nSwitched shunts N/B block pairs NREG extension ID, NREG, name and S/N/B block triples\nTransformer records Four or five rows Expanded winding ratings Expanded winding ratings\nAdditional sections Retained System-wide/switching/substation data retained Voltage droop, switching-rating tables, load types and interfaces retained\n\nThe parser handles comma or whitespace delimiters, single/double quoted fields, doubled quote escapes, commas/slashes inside names, trailing comments, @! headers, empty fields, CRLF, UTF-8 BOM and Fortran D exponents. Optional omitted fields remain absent in .fields; conversion applies documented defaults. Use encoding=:latin1 for legacy extended-ASCII text.\n\nEach PSSERecord retains typed known fields, original token rows and line numbers. PSSERawData.text retains the source text; unknown fields and sections are preserved. Transformer regulation tails are retained as control_fields_1, control_fields_2 and control_fields_3 rather than guessing minor-version node/control-field ordering. Extended bus-name references are retained by the parser; current AC conversion requires numeric bus references.\n\nDelimiter comments determine section transitions when present. Bare zero terminators use the declared revision's section order. Substation internals are opaque, preserving nested records and zero delimiters. Malformed numeric fields, unterminated quotes, truncated transformers, unsupported revisions, premature EOF and data after Q produce source/line errors.","title":"Version handling"},{"category":"section","location":"psse/#Electrical-conversion","page":"PSS/E","text":"The strict AC subset includes energized buses; in-service constant-power loads; fixed shunts; generators (including equal minimum/maximum Q); lines; fixed 2-winding transformer ratios/phases; and switched shunts locked at supplied BINIT. Load impedance components are represented exactly as voltage-squared fixed shunts. Constant-current loads and embedded distributed-generation load models are rejected. Unavailable generators, branches and shunts retain their availability flags. Type-4 buses and their attached equipment are excluded from the energized case, with diagnostics; their original records remain available.\n\nMW/MVAr values are divided by SBASE; angles are converted from degrees to radians. Fixed shunt BL and switched-shunt BINIT retain the positive-capacitive sign. Generator identity is (bus, ID), and branch identity includes (I,J,CKT); integer domain IDs are mapped back through .source_ids.\n\nTransformer CW=1/2/3 and CZ=1/2/3 conversions account for system MVA base, nominal winding kV, winding-bus kV, secondary winding ratio and phase. Tests compare all nine combinations against an independent two-port admittance primitive. Magnetizing admittance and impedance-correction tables are rejected; they are not silently moved to fixed bus shunts that would lose transformer outage association. Nonzero branch-end shunts and bypassed branches are also rejected by conversion.\n\nZero/omitted thermal ratings are not assigned a fabricated finite limit. Conversion requires an explicit unrated_limit in pu and records its use. The source XFRRAT/NXFRAT current-rating unit options are retained, but conversion currently requires MVA ratings. It uses the first normal rating; remaining ratings remain available in the parsed records.\n\nThere must be exactly one energized reference bus for AC conversion. Supplied angles are shifted by its angle. .initial_state retains supplied generator P/Q, including values outside reported limits; only generator optimizer start hints are clamped, with diagnostics. Import success does not certify the source operating point's feasibility.\n\nAutomatic PSS/E voltage regulation, remote control, tap/shunt switching and voltage-droop controllers are not inferred as library droop/AVR controls. Cases with nonempty voltage-droop, DC, FACTS, GNE, induction-machine, switching-device, interface, substation or other unsupported electrical sections are rejected by conversion. Three-winding transformers parse as five-row records but are not yet converted to star equivalents. These records remain accessible in raw.","title":"Electrical conversion"},{"category":"section","location":"psse/#Public-data-and-qualification-limits","page":"PSS/E","text":"Existing public ARPA-E 14- and 500-bus v33 RAW files are exercised without modification.\nA genuine PSS/E 34.6 IEEE 14-bus file is included unchanged under test/data/psse/public_v34_ieee14.raw, with pinned URL, SHA-256 and the upstream MPL-2.0 license in the same directory. Source: VeraGrid fixture.\nA public v33 synthetic South Carolina 500-bus RAW was tested: 500 buses, 90 generators, 466 lines and 131 transformers. Source: PowSyBl public fixture.\nSynthetic v33/v34/v36 fixtures describe the same electrical circuit and test version-specific field positions. No native public v36 exporter file was found in the searched sources. Its present qualification is schema-based; this is not full PSS/E 36 exporter/interoperability certification.\n\nThe implementation references the versioned VeraGrid RAW schema definitions and independently checks transformer conversions against PowerModels' converter equations. V36 additions were cross-checked with the Siemens-authored PSS/E 36.1 Data Formats Reference Manual, sections 1.8–1.13 and RAW format change summary (publicly indexed manual copy); the proprietary manual is not redistributed. In particular, v36 BP and the new sections must not be inferred solely from an inherited v35 branch schema.\n\nEvidence is retained under artifacts/p3_psse_parser_20261022/. This work does not alter sealed P2/S2 or multi-period campaign evidence and does not qualify new controls or security studies.","title":"Public data and qualification limits"},{"category":"section","location":"psse/#Check-a-new-file-against-its-saved-power-flow-state","page":"PSS/E","text":"The ordinary loader now checks the RAW saved solution automatically and logs a compact result. It retains its Case return type. A failed saved-state check warns by default; require_valid=true makes it an acceptance gate:\n\ncase = load_psse_case(\"network.raw\";\n    report_path=\"network.validation.json\", require_valid=true)\n\nThe strict case loader still requires an explicit unrated_limit for missing ratings. To inspect a file without constructing a case for optimization, use the report-only check. This also works when thermal ratings are absent:\n\nreport = check_psse_file(\"network.raw\";\n    report_path=\"network.validation.json\")\nreport[\"power_flow_consistent\"]\nreport[\"known_limits_valid\"]\nreport[\"thermal_coverage_complete\"]\nreport[\"worst_buses\"]\n\nOn the work machine, from the repository directory:\n\njulia --project=. examples/check_psse_file.jl network.raw network.validation.json\n\nExport the solved case from PSS/E to RAW, retaining bus VM/VA and generator PG/QG. The check never re-solves, redispatches, adjusts equipment, clamps the saved state or uploads data. It recomputes both-terminal branch flows, branch losses, shunt consumption and P/Q balance at every bus using the saved values. The JSON report includes source file SHA-256, revision, tolerances, bus mismatches, branch flows, source identities and conversion diagnostics. It refuses to overwrite the source RAW file, including an existing symlink alias.\n\nInterpret the results separately:\n\nStatus Meaning\npassed Saved state satisfies AC balance and the assessed supported normal limits\npower_flow_mismatch At least one bus exceeds the declared P/Q balance tolerance\nlimits_violated AC balance passes but a supported operating limit fails\nlimits_unassessed Balance and known limits pass, but active branches lack ratings\nmissing_saved_solution Required VM/VA or PG/QG fields are absent; defaults cannot certify a saved solution\nunsupported An electrical model cannot be faithfully converted for this check\ninvalid_file File cannot be read or parsed\n\nThe CLI exits 0 only for passed, and 2 for a reported failure or incomplete assessment. Invalid command options or report-write errors remain errors. A solved PSS/E case can balance correctly while violating generator or thermal limits; power_flow_consistent identifies this distinction even when overall valid is false.\n\nDefault balance thresholds are 0.01 MW and 0.01 MVAr per bus, explicitly reported to accommodate common RAW output rounding. They are configurable:\n\nreport = check_psse_file(\"network.raw\";\n    power_tolerance_mw=0.001, reactive_tolerance_mvar=0.001,\n    limit_tolerance_pu=1e-6, top_buses=10)\n\nThese are saved-file comparison thresholds; the library's existing physical solver-acceptance tolerances are unchanged. Loader options use validation_options=(power_tolerance_mw=0.001, reactive_tolerance_mvar=0.001). Automatic checking can be disabled explicitly with check_saved_solution=false.\n\nFor report-only replay, an internal adapter marker lets admittances be evaluated without thermal ratings. That marker is never exposed as a source rating or used to accept thermal loading. Missing ratings appear as JSON null and remain unassessed. No case with such a marker is returned by check_psse_file.\n\nThis checks the supported AC equations at the state saved in RAW. RAW does not supply an independent branch-flow result listing, so recomputed flows are not claimed to have been compared against a separate PSS/E branch-flow export. Original PSS/E solver termination, remote/automatic control semantics, dynamics and security qualification remain outside this small check.","title":"Check a new file against its saved power-flow state"},{"category":"section","location":"robustness/#M4-robustness-and-diagnostics","page":"Robustness","text":"M4 asks a narrower question than scale-up: are the existing small-system M2 and M3 workflows repeatable and diagnostically useful? The implemented milestone provides named SCOPF and M3 design-parameter multi-start experiments, structured operating diagnostics, public-case regressions, reproducible measurements, and an explicit scale-up decision.","title":"M4 robustness and diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"robustness/#Multi-start-comparison","page":"Robustness","text":"solve_scopf_multistart accepts a dictionary from a start name to the usual scenario/state dictionary. An empty dictionary is a cold start. Every run is solved and independently validated; invalid or divergent outcomes are retained.\n\nstarts = Dict(:cold => Dict(), :warm => previous_result.states)\ncomparison = solve_scopf_multistart(study, starts;\n    smooth_epsilon=1e-5, objective_atol=1e-8, objective_rtol=1e-6)\n\nThe classification is one of:\n\n:comparable: at least two independently valid objectives agree within atol + rtol * maximum(abs, objectives);\n:divergent: at least two valid objectives exceed that spread;\n:insufficient_valid_runs: exactly one run is independently valid;\n:no_valid_runs: no run passes validation with a finite objective.\n\nbest_run is selected only among independently valid runs. It is a convenience for follow-on analysis, not evidence that divergent local solutions are interchangeable. The JSON artifact records every labelled solver result, validation report, valid-run set, objective range, and decision.","title":"Multi-start comparison"},{"category":"section","location":"robustness/#Breakpoint-and-binding-diagnostics","page":"Robustness","text":"scopf_diagnostics(study, result) recomputes validation and returns a separate SCOPFDiagnostics artifact. Keeping it separate avoids changing the stable M2 SCOPFReport schema.\n\nFor each active generator control and scenario it records the regulated voltage, exact PWL regime, nearest breakpoint value and index, absolute voltage distance, and near-breakpoint classification. Structured findings cover solver failure, independent-validation failure, near-breakpoint operation, and bus, branch, or generator limits within binding_tolerance.\n\nThe critical contingency is the non-base scenario with the smallest independently recomputed margin across voltage, generator P/Q, and branch thermal limits. This minimum-margin rule is a screening heuristic; it is not a transient or voltage-stability ranking.","title":"Breakpoint and binding diagnostics"},{"category":"section","location":"robustness/#M3-design-parameter-starts","page":"Robustness","text":"optimize_droop_multistart varies actual starting values for slope, voltage reference, and both deadband widths inside the design NLP. Every named input, optimized design, and exact-curve validation report is retained. The result classifies objective agreement and separately reports component-wise final parameter spread, since similar objective values do not prove that the design parameters are identifiable.\n\njulia --project=. examples/m4_design_multistart.jl /tmp/droopopf-m4-design\n\nThe workflow writes design_multistart.json and design_multistart.md.","title":"M3 design-parameter starts"},{"category":"section","location":"robustness/#Reproducible-workflow","page":"Robustness","text":"julia --project=. examples/m4_robustness_workflow.jl /tmp/droopopf-m4\n\nThe workflow compares cold, converged, low-voltage, and high-voltage starts and writes multistart.json, multistart.md, diagnostics.json, and diagnostics.md. It perturbs only initial guesses, not study data or tolerances.","title":"Reproducible workflow"},{"category":"section","location":"robustness/#Public-regressions-and-measurements","page":"Robustness","text":"PGLib-OPF v23.07 case 3 and case 5 are pinned under test/data/pglib with machine-readable CC BY 4.0 provenance. Both run through the normal MATPOWER adapter, control attachment, solver, serialization, and independent validation path. benchmark_scopf records Julia/package/solver identity, structural case size, actual JuMP variable and constraint counts, elapsed time, Julia allocations, termination, objective, and validation status. Peak process RSS is recorded separately in bytes using Sys.maxrss(). The workflow runs each study in a fresh Julia process. RSS includes compilation, warm-up and validation; it is a lifetime high-water mark, not incremental solve memory or a quantity to subtract between samples.\n\nThese are PGLib-derived droop regressions, not reproductions of the published economic OPF benchmarks. DroopOPF uses its schedule-deviation objective and adds one droop controller; the adapter does not enforce MATPOWER angle-difference bounds or use generator costs. The selected cases have no bus shunts or non-unity transformer taps. Source tables and these modeling differences must be distinguished when interpreting results.\n\njulia --project=. examples/m4_benchmark_workflow.jl /tmp/droopopf-m4-benchmark\n\nSee the M4 scale-up decision for the measured evidence and the decision not to select a P2 scaling algorithm from tiny-case data.","title":"Public regressions and measurements"},{"category":"section","location":"global_feasibility/#Optional-global-feasibility-escalation","page":"Global Feasibility","text":"Historical artifacts/ paths on this page identify local campaign records, not files bundled with the release. See archive access and the current qualification index.\n\nGlobal analysis extends the existing three-outcome gate. It does not replace it. Only an independently checked AC point produces feasible; a replayable certificate produces certified_infeasible; all other outcomes are unresolved.\n\nThe scalable policy is to replay known points first, attempt local feasibility, then escalate selected unresolved cases. Do not make full global search a mandatory cost for every candidate, demand condition or contingency.","title":"Optional global feasibility escalation"},{"category":"section","location":"global_feasibility/#Implemented-layers","page":"Global Feasibility","text":"Layer Model/evidence Can establish feasibility? Can certify infeasibility?\nExisting gate Original AC/PWL replay and analytical bounds Yes Yes, for supported analytical contradictions\nSparse outer LP Voltage-square envelopes, edge-product SOC support cuts, terminal thermal cuts, full droop convex hull No Yes, with exact rational Farkas replay\nCycle PSD cuts Bounded-size matrix blocks with shared fill products and exact rational support cuts No Yes, through the same rational LP certificate checker\nLP bound tightening Min/max selected voltages, exact weak-duality bounds, rebuilt square/droop envelopes No Yes, if the refined LP yields a replayable contradiction\nSparse SOC relaxation Edge products, voltage-square envelopes, full droop convex hull No Numerical evidence only in this implementation\nGlobal search Rectangular AC equations, lifted edge flows, exact SOS2 droop regions Yes, after original-model replay Numerical solver evidence only; complete search-tree proof verification is not implemented\n\nAll models include generator availability, the study P/Q bounds, fixed source taps/shunts, both branch-terminal ratings and the frozen feasibility tolerances. Selected/free equipment is explicitly unsupported by these global backends; the gate records that limitation rather than freezing it silently. The verified LP currently requires zero branch phase shifts, which both imported test cases satisfy. It deliberately omits angle windows and rank-one constraints, so its feasible set contains the original feasible set. Optional cycle PSD cuts strengthen consistency between edges while retaining a valid outer relaxation.\n\nThe SOC/global models include supplied edge angle windows within (-pi/2,pi/2). Other windows are recorded as omitted. Edge angles are periodic, and one gauge is chosen per island; original unwrapped angles and reference requirements are always rechecked on returned points. Consequently these restrictions can be an outer representation of the original angle contract. They cannot exclude an original feasible point by silently selecting a droop region.\n\nGlobal search uses thermal-current bounds and shortest-path angle bounds to narrow rectangular voltage coordinates, with numerical outward padding. These numerical bounds are not themselves accepted as exact certificates.","title":"Implemented layers"},{"category":"section","location":"global_feasibility/#What-makes-an-LP-certificate-independently-checkable","page":"Global Feasibility","text":"For the outer LP Ax <= b, with finite box bounds l <= x <= u, the LP solver suggests nonnegative multipliers y. The checker computes, using exact rational arithmetic on stored inputs,\n\nm = sum_i min(A^mathsfTy)_i l_i\n                        (A^mathsfTy)_i u_i-b^mathsfTy\n\nIf m > 0, every point in the box contradicts the weighted inequalities. This remains valid when the numerical multiplier vector has a stationarity residual: the box correction accounts for it instead of assuming it is zero.\n\nNetwork admittances for zero phase shifts are constructed rationally from stored R, X, charging and tap data. Physical PWL knots are retained. SOC and thermal support directions are checked to have norm at most one. Floating-point LP rows are rounded outwards over their finite boxes. A certificate reader reconstructs the mathematical relaxation and verifies its positive rational contradiction; it does not trust HiGHS's infeasibility status.\n\nVoltage-bound tightening uses the corresponding weak-duality formula with the objective coefficient included. Each improved bound has its own multiplier proof. Bounds are rounded outwards to a dyadic grid to control arithmetic size. Every proof is replayed before rebuilding the droop graph's convex hull on a narrower domain. The ledger's displayed floating-point bounds are not evidence.\n\nverified_feasibility_voltage_bounds can supply these bounds to a subsequent global search. They apply to the original strict problem: passing them to a model that relaxes bus voltage is rejected. Use max_relaxation=0 for that search.","title":"What makes an LP certificate independently checkable"},{"category":"section","location":"global_feasibility/#Bounded-cycle-strengthening","page":"Global Feasibility","text":"Pass psd_clique_limit=8 to certify_feasibility_lp to enable cycle strengthening. The default remains zero for compatibility with earlier ledgers. A deterministic minimum-degree elimination builds small cliques and shared auxiliary voltage products for fill edges. Elimination stops if the next clique would exceed the limit (supported limits: 3–32). Any remaining dense core is left relaxed. Parallel circuits share their bus-voltage products, with conjugation for reversed edges.\n\nFor a physical voltage vector, the lifted matrix is W = V V*, so every principal block is positive semidefinite. At an LP solution, negative eigenvectors suggest cuts of the form z* W z >= 0. The vector is quantized to rational components; every such vector gives a valid cut, whether or not it is an accurate eigenvector. Replay reconstructs its exact rational coefficients and the clique from the network. Numerical eigenvalues are never accepted as proof.\n\nThis also covers cycles longer than triangles by introducing shared fill products. It does not impose rank one or complete cycle consistency. Cuts are added only as needed and obey the existing round/time budgets. Bound-tightening stages and saved certificates retain the clique configuration, so they rebuild the same sparse variables and inequalities. Older ledgers still reconstruct the original model with no cycle extension.\n\nlp = certify_feasibility_lp(problem; optimizer_factory=HiGHS.Optimizer,\n    psd_clique_limit=8, time_limit=60.0, max_rounds=100,\n    bound_tightening_rounds=3, bound_bus_limit=16)","title":"Bounded cycle strengthening"},{"category":"section","location":"global_feasibility/#Optional-solver-environment","page":"Global Feasibility","text":"The main package has no new solver dependencies. From the repository root:\n\njulia --project=examples/global_feasibility_env -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.develop(path=\".\"); Pkg.instantiate()'\njulia --project=examples/global_feasibility_env examples/s1_arpae14_global_feasibility.jl\njulia --project=examples/global_feasibility_env examples/s1_arpae14_global_tightening.jl\njulia --project=examples/global_feasibility_env examples/global_feasibility_scale_probe.jl\njulia --project=examples/global_feasibility_env examples/s1_cycle_feasibility.jl\njulia --project=examples/global_feasibility_env examples/s1_cycle_global_search.jl\n\nThe optional environment uses HiGHS for LP search and SCIP for global search. SCIP supports spatial branch-and-cut for bounded MINLPs; this provides numerical global search, not an independently replayable nonlinear search-tree certificate in DroopOPF. See SCIP's problem classes and the Julia interface.\n\nresult = check_feasibility(problem;\n    initial_states = [source_state],\n    lp_options = (optimizer_factory=HiGHS.Optimizer,\n                  time_limit=30.0, bound_tightening_rounds=2),\n    global_options = (optimizer_factory=SCIP.Optimizer,\n                      target_bus=8, time_limit=60.0),\n)\n\nTo reuse verified bounds explicitly:\n\nlp = certify_feasibility_lp(problem; optimizer_factory=HiGHS.Optimizer,\n    time_limit=30.0, bound_tightening_rounds=2)\nif lp.status == :unresolved\n    global_analysis = solve_global_feasibility(problem;\n        optimizer_factory=SCIP.Optimizer, target_bus=8, max_relaxation=0.0,\n        voltage_bound_result=lp, time_limit=60.0)\nend\n\nBudgets cap LP refinement rounds, bound-tightening rounds, the number of buses selected for tightening, and solver time. Model compilation/construction and independent proof replay have additional overhead. Solver bounds, candidate points, controls, scope fingerprints and failed attempts remain in the ledgers.","title":"Optional solver environment"},{"category":"section","location":"global_feasibility/#Current-14-bus-evidence-and-500-bus-scale-probe","page":"Global Feasibility","text":"At 90% demand with source taps/shunts fixed, both corrected droop variants remain unresolved after sparse relaxations, three rounds of verified bound tightening, and bounded global searches. The voltage-restoration searches retained a zero global lower bound at their 60-second limits. Positive local relaxation values therefore remain upper bounds, not infeasibility certificates.\n\nThe strict searches after verified bound tightening also have a separate ledger; no relaxed point can pass as a strict witness. The scripts preserve the original OPF/SCOPF files and start no SCOPF campaigns.\n\nThe 500-bus probe imports all 90 generator records, including an unavailable zero-Q-range unit, and calibrates 51 active no-deadband curves to the saved OPF. It builds the sparse SOC and global models and independently replays that source point. It runs only a bounded LP sanity check, not a 500-bus global proof search. The global model has 5,951 variables for 593 active branches, demonstrating sparse construction; this does not establish tractable global solution time.\n\nResults are in artifacts/s1_arpae14_global_feasibility/ and artifacts/s1_global_scale_probe/. Cycle-strengthened results are in artifacts/s1_cycle_feasibility/. The cycle LP uses 11 small PSD blocks and 24 lifted edges on the 14-bus case. Three verified bound-tightening rounds improve voltage bounds, but both 90% demand variants remain unresolved. Each strict global search using the new verified voltage bounds reached its 60-second limit with no feasible witness and no infeasibility proof (numerical lower bound zero). The 500-bus cycle model has 236 small PSD blocks, 888 lifted edges (including fill), and 3,166 LP variables; construction was approximately 0.48 seconds in the measured warm run. Its saved source point passed independent replay and the new PSD separation check. A 60-second LP budget remained inconclusive, as expected for a known feasible source point; this LP is not itself a feasibility witness.\n\nThe cycle and previous LP runs used different refinement budgets, so their bound comparison records the combined effect of cuts and refinement, not an isolated performance benchmark. Larger cases retain the same gate and bounded escalation policy; sparse construction alone does not demonstrate tractable global proof search.","title":"Current 14-bus evidence and 500-bus scale probe"},{"category":"section","location":"global_feasibility/#API","page":"Global Feasibility","text":"","title":"API"},{"category":"function","location":"global_feasibility/#DroopOPF.build_global_feasibility_model","page":"Global Feasibility","text":"Build a bounded voltage-restoration model for a frozen fixed-equipment problem.\n\nmode=:global uses rectangular AC voltages and exact SOS2 droop regions. mode=:soc uses sparse edge-product SOC envelopes and droop graph convex hulls. Only target_bus may exceed its original upper voltage bound, by delta in [0,max_relaxation]. Frozen feasibility tolerances are included in both models. The SOC model is an outer relaxation, never a feasible-point certificate. Angle windows outside (-pi/2,pi/2) are explicitly omitted (an outer relaxation). Pass an optional global/conic solver factory; no optimization occurs here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.build_global_feasibility_model"},{"category":"function","location":"global_feasibility/#DroopOPF.seed_global_feasibility!","page":"Global Feasibility","text":"Map an AC point to every lifted variable and SOS2 weight, without changing the problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.seed_global_feasibility!"},{"category":"function","location":"global_feasibility/#DroopOPF.solve_global_feasibility","page":"Global Feasibility","text":"Run one explicitly budgeted global/SOC escalation and return its gate result and ledger.\n\nOnly independently replayed AC points can upgrade the gate here. Floating-point solver bounds are retained as solver_reported_infeasibility evidence, not promoted to independently verified certificates. This distinction also holds at time limits and when the solver reports OPTIMAL/INFEASIBLE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solve_global_feasibility"},{"category":"function","location":"global_feasibility/#DroopOPF.certify_feasibility_lp","page":"Global Feasibility","text":"Try a sparse outer LP and return only independently verified infeasibility.\n\nThe optional LP solver supplies candidate Farkas multipliers. The checker uses exact rational arithmetic, including residual stationarity errors over finite variable bounds. SOC/thermal tangent cuts are independently validated. Optional psd_clique_limit (0 disables; 3–32 enables) adds bounded-size cycle PSD support cuts, whose rational coefficients are reconstructed independently. Feasible LP points never qualify the original nonconvex problem. The work is bounded by time_limit and max_rounds; no global solver is required by the main package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.certify_feasibility_lp"},{"category":"function","location":"global_feasibility/#DroopOPF.verified_feasibility_voltage_bounds","page":"Global Feasibility","text":"Replay every LP weak-duality bound proof and return outward-rounded voltage bounds.\n\nThis never trusts the ledger's displayed bounds. Bounds from the original strict problem must not be reused as bounds on a voltage-relaxed problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.verified_feasibility_voltage_bounds"},{"category":"section","location":"equipment_optimization/#M7-equipment-and-joint-droop-optimization","page":"Equipment Optimization","text":"These synthetic three-bus studies demonstrate base-case continuous optimization. They do not establish security-constrained equipment performance, discrete implementability or scalability. All three slices use the same dispatch-deviation objective; losses and setting deviations are separate metrics.\n\nThe joint API also accepts encoding=:complementarity to optimize the same continuous tap, simple-bank and bounded droop-parameter variables with CCOpt's exact droop graph. This provides an exact-versus-smoothed droop comparison without changing the equipment relaxation, bounds, or objective. Omitted droop parameter bounds keep those parameters fixed, as in the smooth path. The returned result records both encoding and complementarity_residual_max. Discrete tap positions and switched-shunt selection are not part of this comparison.\n\nA reproducible matched run is provided by examples/m7_ccopt_comparison.jl. It writes each solver's complete design, independent physical residuals, setting differences and objective differences to artifacts/m7_ccopt_comparison.\n\nThe dedicated optimize_taps and optimize_shunts APIs accept the same encoding=:complementarity option. Their result records include encoding and complementarity_residual_max, matching joint-design reporting. As with joint design, omit optimizer_factory in exact mode and pass CCOpt settings through optimizer_attributes.\n\nThe tables below summarize historical synthetic studies. A selected tap-sweep figure is included in this documentation. Full inputs, gallery figures and reports for m7_1, m7_2 and m7_3 are in the local research archives; restore those prerequisites before using examples/build_m7_gallery.py.","title":"M7 equipment and joint droop optimization"},{"category":"section","location":"equipment_optimization/#M7.1-—-Transformer-taps","page":"Equipment Optimization","text":"Fixed-bound and standalone equivalence, a 41-point tap sweep and one/two-free-tap comparisons. Transformer phase shifts, shunts and droops stay fixed.\n\nConfiguration Objective Tap 11 AC residual (pu) Branch loss (pu)\nfixed 2.760045e-05 1.02 2.431215e-15 0.002264238\ntap 11 free 1.994948e-05 1.0023 2.870759e-13 0.002113501\ntaps 11 and 22 free 1.993398e-05 1.001548 5.830753e-14 0.002108155\n\nArchived full evidence and restoration instructions","title":"M7.1 — Transformer taps"},{"category":"section","location":"equipment_optimization/#M7.2-—-Simple-capacitor/reactor-banks","page":"Equipment Optimization","text":"Each bank has one step type. A fractional count scales G and B together. Taps and droops stay fixed. Each capacitor/reactor sweep contains 31 points. Complex-bank optimization is deferred until simple-bank/transformer/droop scaling is demonstrated.\n\nEquipment Mode Objective AC residual (pu) Branch loss (pu) Shunt active consumption (pu)\ncapacitor fixed 3.165313e-05 3.969047e-15 0.002262357 0.003334059\ncapacitor one free 2.935443e-05 2.124758e-09 0.002287727 0.002377693\ncapacitor both free 2.557165e-05 2.245834e-11 0.002289527 0.001899371\nreactor fixed 3.726789e-05 5.172945e-15 0.002329133 0.003314389\nreactor one free 2.935434e-05 1.236122e-12 0.002287783 0.002376528\nreactor both free 2.557165e-05 6.664602e-11 0.002289591 0.001898015\n\nArchived full evidence and restoration instructions","title":"M7.2 — Simple capacitor/reactor banks"},{"category":"section","location":"equipment_optimization/#M7.3-—-Joint-equipment-and-droop-design","page":"Equipment Optimization","text":"Digits denote tap / shunt / droop, with 1 free and 0 fixed. Each configuration has two starts. Tables select the lowest-objective physically valid attempt; the evidence retains every attempt and failure. Tap 11 is bounded to [0.95,1.05], bank 201 B to [0,0.06] pu, and droop 2 slope to [0.04,0.10]. Other parameters are fixed in this grid; separate regression tests exercise reference and asymmetric deadband selection.\n\nT/S/D Valid starts Objective Tap B (pu) Slope AC residual (pu)\n000 2/2 3.165313e-05 1.02 0.02 0.075 1.915135e-15\n001 2/2 2.857831e-05 1.02 0.02 0.0999999 8.430756e-16\n010 2/2 2.935435e-05 1.02 3.024316e-05 0.075 1.826502e-10\n011 2/2 2.620771e-05 1.02 1.061755e-07 0.0999999 1.034173e-13\n100 2/2 2.37211e-05 1.002058 0.02 0.075 1.460221e-13\n101 2/2 2.37124e-05 1.000147 0.02 0.05446868 1.136674e-12\n110 2/2 2.183084e-05 1.002401 4.454692e-05 0.075 7.189332e-09\n111 2/2 2.18081e-05 0.9993228 1.480988e-07 0.04644218 1.709433e-11\n\nT/S/D Active objective Reactive objective Branch loss Shunt consumption Objective spread Slope spread\n000 1.565994e-05 1.599318e-05 0.002262357 0.003334059 3.455894e-19 0\n001 1.557982e-05 1.299849e-05 0.002256893 0.003325188 5.23072e-06 0.05999455\n010 1.088287e-05 1.847148e-05 0.002287728 0.002377647 7.98245e-11 0\n011 1.081902e-05 1.538869e-05 0.002282338 0.002369333 1.770052e-10 4.499464e-09\n100 1.489327e-05 8.827828e-06 0.002114444 0.003343261 2.78335e-17 0\n101 1.490091e-05 8.811489e-06 0.002110516 0.003348588 1.378631e-17 2.530132e-09\n110 1.024779e-05 1.158305e-05 0.002141423 0.002385782 6.579897e-11 0\n111 1.024086e-05 1.156724e-05 0.002135656 0.002390017 2.098927e-15 2.75197e-07\n\nThe objective is the sum of the two listed components. Design penalty is zero in every configuration. Loss quantities are in pu. The second-start design values are tap=1, B=0.04 and slope=0.095 for enabled families, with the solved fixed operating state; the first start uses supplied settings and the declared proportional-regime state.","title":"M7.3 — Joint equipment and droop design"},{"category":"section","location":"equipment_optimization/#Matched-attribution-and-interactions","page":"Equipment Optimization","text":"Each comparison below changes only droop freedom. Variation across these four benefits measures its dependence on equipment freedom; isolated benefits must not be added without accounting for interactions.\n\nEquipment freedom held fixed Objective decrease from freeing droop\ntap=0 shunt=0 3.074821e-06\ntap=0 shunt=1 3.146635e-06\ntap=1 shunt=0 8.702212e-09\ntap=1 shunt=1 2.274399e-08\n\nSome starts may reach different valid local solutions. Similar objectives with different settings indicate weak parameter identification on this case. Multi-start evidence is not a global-optimality certificate.\n\nArchived full evidence and restoration instructions","title":"Matched attribution and interactions"},{"category":"section","location":"equipment_optimization/#Acceptance-and-next-scope","page":"Equipment Optimization","text":"Independent AC and exact-droop tolerances are 1e-6 and 1e-5 pu. Smooth epsilon is 1e-5. Fixed/free comparisons allow objective differences of 1e-8 for numerical equivalence. The full suite checks policy perturbations, physical replay, serialization and Ipopt/MadNLP agreement. M8 adds steady-state transformer AVR; M9 adds coordinated equipment decisions and response policies across contingencies.","title":"Acceptance and next scope"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Solver-formulations","page":"Solvers","text":"","title":"Solver formulations"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Smooth-nonlinear-programming","page":"Solvers","text":"solve_opf encodes the positive-part terms in the volt-var graph with stable softplus functions based on LogExpFunctions.log1pexp. Ipopt is the default:\n\nipopt_result = solve_opf(case; smooth_epsilon = 1.0e-3)\n\nMadNLP receives the same JuMP model and can be selected through the optimizer factory:\n\nusing MadNLP\nmadnlp_result = solve_opf(\n    case;\n    optimizer_factory = MadNLP.Optimizer,\n    smooth_epsilon = 1.0e-3,\n)\n\nThe voltage smoothing width is in per-unit voltage. By default, reactive smoothing is a relative fraction of each control's available reactive range; smooth_reactive_epsilon can override it with an absolute per-unit width.","title":"Smooth nonlinear programming"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Exact-complementarity-formulation","page":"Solvers","text":"solve_opf_complementarity uses MathOptComplements and CCOpt. Each voltage hinge is represented as a nonnegative complementarity pair. Reactive clipping is represented by the KKT conditions for projection of the raw droop value onto the control's reactive interval.\n\nWhen both deadband widths are fixed at exactly zero, the raw response is Qraw = Qref + (Vref - V) / slope. The model omits the two voltage-hinge pairs and their four auxiliary variables; the two Q-projection pairs remain. This applies to fixed curves and to designs with free slope/reference and fixed zero widths. Positive or variable widths retain the general hinge formulation. The smooth backend uses the same affine raw response and retains its reactive saturation smoothing. This is algebraically equivalent to subtracting the two voltage softplus terms, including derivatives.\n\nccopt_encoding_audit marks these controllers with voltage_hinges_eliminated=true; their hinge errors are zero by construction. Raw-response, projection and independent exact-droop errors remain reported.\n\nccopt_result = solve_opf_complementarity(case)\n\nThe exact formulation has no smoothing parameter. CCOpt may report ALMOST_LOCALLY_SOLVED after its homotopy; use the independent equilibrium report and complementarity_residual_max to assess the result.","title":"Exact complementarity formulation"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Comparing-solutions","page":"Solvers","text":"Ipopt and MadNLP should usually agree closely because they solve the same smoothed NLP. CCOpt solves the exact PWL graph, so compare physical residuals, objectives, operating regimes, and state differences rather than expecting bitwise equality. The reproducible comparison is:\n\njulia --project=. examples/m1_solver_comparison.jl\n\nSee Solver compatibility for recommended starting tolerances.","title":"Comparing solutions"},{"category":"section","location":"solvers/#Milestone-compatibility-matrix","page":"Solvers","text":"Capability Ipopt MadNLP CCOpt\nM1 single-case OPF smooth, tested smooth, tested exact, tested\nM2 fixed-curve SCOPF smooth, tested smooth, tested with a validated warm start exact, tested\nM3 bounded parameter design smooth, tested smooth, tested with a validated warm start not a design optimizer\nM3 reconstructed fixed curve smooth re-solve available smooth re-solve available exact re-solve, tested\n\nThe M2/M3 fixture is nonconvex and MadNLP can select an infeasible local point from its default start at the tight 1e-5 smoothing width. The compatibility test therefore warm-starts MadNLP from the independently validated Ipopt M2 states. Both smooth solvers then validate and reach the same M3 objective, but their optimized parameter values need not match because the design is weakly identifiable.\n\noptimize_droop_parameters rejects CCOpt.Optimizer: its parameterized model is a smooth NLP, not a complementarity formulation. After reconstruction with with_droop_settings, use validate_droop_design for exact PWL state replay, which is independent of all three solvers, or use solve_scopf(...; encoding=:complementarity) for a CCOpt exact fixed-curve re-solve. Solver termination alone is never the acceptance test; the resulting state must pass equilibrium_report.","title":"Milestone compatibility matrix"},{"category":"section","location":"research/#Optional-research-evidence","page":"Research evidence","text":"The software release does not include the historical artifacts/ tree. Its contents, including failed attempts, have been preserved in verified local archives by the maintainer. Those archives are not public release assets. They are not required to install, test or use the library. Archive publication is not part of this release, at the maintainer’s request.\n\nThe following inventory documents the local preservation format; it is not a list of downloads supplied with this release.\n\nAsset Contents\ncampaigns.tar.gz Full historical campaign outputs and sealed manifests\nresearch-docs.tar.gz Original study reports, figure galleries, roadmap and historical notes\nresearch-drivers.tar.gz Exact source, tests, full study inputs, example drivers and environment snapshot\nevidence-index.json SHA-256 archive checksums, file counts and verification status\n*-files.json Original relative paths and per-file SHA-256 hashes\n\nArchives preserve repository-relative paths. Restore them into a separate research directory, not over an installed release: historical source and docs can differ from the release. Verify archive checksums before extraction, then verify individual files against the corresponding file index. Sealed campaign manifests provide additional original verification. Some old scripts contain machine-specific paths; restoration does not promise turnkey reruns of every historical workflow. No campaign runs during package installation or tests.\n\nThe latest evidence is artifacts/p2_targeted_relaxations_20261023/REPORT.md: 389/389 connected contingencies, five periods, 390 selected result files replayed, unchanged P2 physical limits. Earlier reports retain their contemporary failures and policies; use the release scope for current status.\n\nS1/S2/P1/P2/P3 are study and development labels, not required downloads. The original S2 campaign remains paused. Archived source snapshots are retained for reproducibility, not as alternative supported implementations.","title":"Optional research evidence"},{"category":"section","location":"research/#Restore-a-local-archived-campaign","page":"Research evidence","text":"The maintainer can restore a campaign into a new directory without overwriting an existing study:\n\npython3 tools/release/restore_campaign.py /path/to/research-v0.10.0-rc1 \\\n  psse_saved_solution_check_20261022 /path/to/restored-campaign\n\nThe command verifies the archive SHA-256 and every selected file before making the restored directory available. It refuses an existing destination.\n\nA small, curated validation reference gallery is bundled with the documentation. It contains selected summaries and figures, not full research archives, network inputs or solver logs. The historical roadmap is archived; the public milestone index provides current navigation.","title":"Restore a local archived campaign"},{"category":"section","location":"milestones/#Milestone-and-evidence-index","page":"Milestones","text":"Implementation, numerical qualification and research closure are separate statuses. The release scope records the current supported boundaries. Original campaign policies remain attached to their results; later success does not rewrite an earlier failure or migrate historical S2 results to P2 semantics.\n\nMilestone Documentation / evidence Status and boundary\nM1–M4: OPF, SCOPF, droop design and robustness SCOPF, droop design, robustness Historical implementation milestones completed within their declared small-case scope\nM5–M6: transformer and shunt models Data model Implemented static equipment models; original numerical evidence archived\nM7: continuous equipment and joint droop design Equipment studies, selected figures Synthetic base-case comparisons; continuous settings, local solutions\nM8–M9: equipment control and coordinated security design Data model, SCOPF Steady-state control models and declared security policies; discrete actions and dynamics remain separate\nM10: contingency assessment and comparative evidence Assessment workflow, security design Complete for declared assessment/comparison scope; not general network security certification\nS1: convergence and scale Release scope, archived S1 closeout Closed within declared sampled 14/500-bus base-operation scope\nS2: shared security design Release scope, archived S2 reports Declared 14-bus scope closed; original 500-bus campaign paused\nP1: research workflow Validation, robustness Implementation and declared small-case workflow evidence retained\nP2: scaling and qualification Mixed controls, latest reference report Latest mixed 500-bus design passes all 389 connected outages at five periods; broader P2 remains open\nP3: extended models Multi-period, PSS/E, mixed controls Implemented slices; native RAW v36 export qualification, storage, detailed GFL/GFM and dynamics remain open","title":"Milestone and evidence index"},{"category":"section","location":"milestones/#Evidence-structure","page":"Milestones","text":"Each submilestone should identify its scope and assumptions, model equations, runnable example, machine-readable results, readable report and specified figures/tables. Acceptance separates native solver termination, physical replay, operating limits and control-policy compliance. A numerical failure is unresolved unless separate evidence establishes infeasibility under the stated contract.\n\nSealed campaigns additionally record input/source hashes and output manifests. Historical layouts vary; not every early milestone has the same folder template. The research archive guide explains how to recover full records.\n\nFor future reports, record the date, source revision, input identity, network and contingency coverage, objective, control sharing, movement/timing assumptions, acceptance tolerances, rejected attempts, independent replay and remaining gates. Keep new campaigns separate when any physical or information policy changes.","title":"Evidence structure"},{"category":"section","location":"milestones/#Documentation-maintenance","page":"Milestones","text":"Update this index and the release scope when qualification changes. Keep historical outcomes labelled by campaign; link current summaries to compact evidence with provenance. Package CI and documentation builds establish software integrity, not renewed acceptance of every archived large-network experiment.","title":"Documentation maintenance"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-reference","page":"API","text":"","title":"API reference"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.AVR","page":"API","text":"Automatic voltage regulation with generator Q limits supplied by the case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.AVR"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.Branch","page":"API","text":"Two-terminal line or transformer with a from-side complex tap tap_ratio * cis(phase_shift). kind is explicit :line or :transformer metadata; it does not alter the electrical equations. The ratio is positive and dimensionless; phase shift is in radians. Ratio and phase are fixed equipment inputs in M5.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.Branch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ComplementarityOPFResult","page":"API","text":"Result returned by solve_opf_complementarity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ComplementarityOPFResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.Contingency","page":"API","text":"A named outage overlay. Device IDs refer to the original case; :base is reserved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.Contingency"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyAssessment","page":"API","text":"One independently validated fixed-base evaluation from a contingency catalog.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyAssessment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyCatalog","page":"API","text":"A deterministic, provenance-preserving N-1 candidate set for one case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyCatalog"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyCatalogEntry","page":"API","text":"One candidate N-1 outage together with its structural disposition.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyCatalogEntry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyFilterDecision","page":"API","text":"One conservative pre-solve routing decision.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyFilterDecision"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyPipelineAssessment","page":"API","text":"One routed contingency and any evaluation retained for it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyPipelineAssessment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyPipelineRank","page":"API","text":"Unified operational rank for screened, exact, and island-aware outcomes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyPipelineRank"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyPipelineResult","page":"API","text":"Complete, coverage-checked result of the contingency assessment pipeline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyPipelineResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyRank","page":"API","text":"One exact-evaluation severity ranking; it never asserts that omitted cases are safe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyRank"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ContingencyScreeningScore","page":"API","text":"A cheap pre-solve priority score; it is not a safety classification.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ContingencyScreeningScore"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopBreakpointDiagnostic","page":"API","text":"Distance from one active controller operating point to its nearest PWL breakpoint.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopBreakpointDiagnostic"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopControl","page":"API","text":"Independent bounds for the existing M3 droop family; omitted fields stay fixed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopControl"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopDesignMultiStartResult","page":"API","text":"M3 parameter multi-start comparison retaining every design attempt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopDesignMultiStartResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopDesignMultiStartRun","page":"API","text":"Optimized M3 design and independent validation from one parameter start.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopDesignMultiStartRun"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopDesignResult","page":"API","text":"M3 optimized settings, reference objective, and scenario-indexed SCOPF result.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopDesignResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopParameterSpread","page":"API","text":"Component-wise spread across independently valid optimized settings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopParameterSpread"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopQualification","page":"API","text":"Candidate droops and all required calibration/demand feasibility decisions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopQualification"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopSecurityIteration","page":"API","text":"One design round, including all executed independent assessments and errors.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopSecurityIteration"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopSecurityResult","page":"API","text":"Contingency-addition result; eligible acceptance and full N-1 coverage differ.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopSecurityResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopSettings","page":"API","text":"Physical settings for the optimizable standard volt-var droop family.\n\ndeadband_low and deadband_high are nonnegative widths below and above v_ref; they may differ or both be fixed at zero for a no-deadband curve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopSettings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopSlopeSweep","page":"API","text":"A reference-anchored sweep of one control's positive droop slope.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopSlopeSweep"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.DroopSlopeSweepPoint","page":"API","text":"Metrics and solver status for one fixed droop slope in an M3 parameter sweep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.DroopSlopeSweepPoint"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.EquilibriumValidationReport","page":"API","text":"Independent checks for a solved AC equilibrium.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.EquilibriumValidationReport"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.FeasibilityProblem","page":"API","text":"A frozen, base-case feasibility contract. Omitted taps/banks remain fixed.\n\nSelected banks are continuous susceptances within their specified envelope. angle_limits maps branch IDs to (minimum, maximum) bus-angle differences in radians. All physical checks use exact PWL droops, regardless of search encoding. The constructor copies the inputs; subsequent mutation of the snapshot is rejected. This is not a coupled SCOPF contract.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.FeasibilityProblem"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.FeasibilityResult","page":"API","text":"Tri-state decision and its witness or replayable certificate, with a search ledger.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.FeasibilityResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.FeasibilityWitness","page":"API","text":"An AC state and the actual equipment settings at that state; no solver claim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.FeasibilityWitness"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.FixedQ","page":"API","text":"Generator Q is fixed to an explicit schedule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.FixedQ"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.FixedShunt","page":"API","text":"Fixed bus admittance in per unit: G >= 0 consumes P; B > 0 injects Q. Terminal consumption is (conductance - im*susceptance)*abs2(V). Availability disables the equipment without losing its supplied parameters. This is not a switched-bank model; no legal positions or controls are inferred.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.FixedShunt"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.FreeQ","page":"API","text":"Generator Q is bounded only by its declared capability.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.FreeQ"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.IslandSelection","page":"API","text":"Selection and accounting for the component retained after an islanding outage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.IslandSelection"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.IslandedContingencyAssessment","page":"API","text":"Reduced-network OPF and independent validation for one islanding outage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.IslandedContingencyAssessment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.MultiPeriodResult","page":"API","text":"Native multi-period result: per-period states/loss MW, shared droop settings, and total duration-weighted loss MWh. Acceptance requires validate_multi_period.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.MultiPeriodResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.MultiPeriodSecurityResult","page":"API","text":"Multi-period AC security result. Contingency states are settled responses; there is no transient trajectory or inter-period ramp on outage states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.MultiPeriodSecurityResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.MultiPeriodStudy","page":"API","text":"Ordered, deterministic AC periods with fixed equipment and common droop curves.\n\nduration_hours[t] weights period t's energy and is the elapsed time from period t to t+1. ramp_limits[id] = (up, down) is in pu/hour on the case MVA base. Omitted generators have no inter-period ramp restriction. There is no initial/terminal boundary, storage, commitment, uncertainty or outage recourse. Only loads may differ between period cases; their IDs and buses must agree.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.MultiPeriodStudy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.PSSEImport","page":"API","text":"Strict AC import plus unmodified supplied operating-point data and source-ID maps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.PSSEImport"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.PSSERawData","page":"API","text":"Parsed full-case RAW revision 33, 34 or 36; source text and unknown sections are retained. This is bus/branch RAW, not binary SAV, DYR, SEQ or RAWX. Parsing does not certify that every equipment model can be represented by the DroopOPF AC domain.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.PSSERawData"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.PSSERecord","page":"API","text":"One PSS/E RAW record, with typed known fields, original token rows and source lines. Opaque sections retain token rows without assigning electrical semantics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.PSSERecord"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.PiecewiseLinearCurve","page":"API","text":"A strictly increasing, piecewise-linear scalar curve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.PiecewiseLinearCurve"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ReactiveControlAssignment","page":"API","text":"Attach one explicit reactive-control mode to a generator.\n\nFreeQ and FixedQ act at the generator and therefore take no regulated location. AVR and VoltVarDroop require a declared voltage location.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ReactiveControlAssignment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ReactiveControlMode","page":"API","text":"Declared reactive-control semantics for one generator.\n\nThese modes are deliberately independent of their numerical encoding. AVR may later be represented by a smooth transition or exact complementarity without changing the physical assignment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ReactiveControlMode"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFBenchmarkReport","page":"API","text":"Reproducible environment, structural size, and samples for one SCOPF study.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFBenchmarkReport"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFBenchmarkSample","page":"API","text":"One measured SCOPF solve, including actual JuMP model size and allocations.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFBenchmarkSample"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFControlPolicy","page":"API","text":"SCOPFControlPolicy(; taps=:corrective, shunts=:corrective, reactive=:automatic,\n    tap_movement_limits=nothing, shunt_movement_limits=nothing)\n\nDeclare cross-scenario timing. Taps and shunts default to corrective settings selected independently within their control bounds. Preventive equipment has one shared setting. Reactive assignments and voltage targets remain shared, while Q and voltage respond to each scenario's equations and availability.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFControlPolicy"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFDiagnostics","page":"API","text":"Structured SCOPF diagnostics kept separate from the stable validation report.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFDiagnostics"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFDroopDesignResult","page":"API","text":"Shared multi-controller droop design and its ordinary SCOPF scenario states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFDroopDesignResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFFinding","page":"API","text":"A machine-readable M4 finding tied to a scenario and optional network object.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFFinding"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFMultiStartResult","page":"API","text":"Comparison of named SCOPF starts without discarding divergent or invalid runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFMultiStartResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFMultiStartRun","page":"API","text":"One independently solved and validated SCOPF start.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFMultiStartRun"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFReport","page":"API","text":"Independent per-scenario physical and inter-scenario response checks.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFReport"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFResult","page":"API","text":"Scenario-indexed SCOPF output; :base is always present. States may be missing on failure. balancing_power stores the common active-response request signal; after saturation it differs from total delivered adjustment and can be nonunique.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFResult"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.SCOPFStageAssessment","page":"API","text":"Paired immediate and settled steady-state SCOPF assessments sharing one fixed base.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.SCOPFStageAssessment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ShuntBank","page":"API","text":"Shunt bank with explicitly supplied legal step-count combinations. Step admittances use the fixed-shunt G/B convention. State and nominal_state are integer count tuples, not optimization variables. Metadata is copied to immutable tuples; unavailable banks retain their legal state but contribute zero admittance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ShuntBank"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ShuntControl","page":"API","text":"Select a simple bank for continuous susceptance optimization.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ShuntControl"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.Study","page":"API","text":"Study(case; contingencies=[], mode=:preventive, participation=nothing, redispatch_limits=Dict(),\n      tap_controls=[], shunt_controls=[], control_policy=SCOPFControlPolicy())\n\nFixed-curve SCOPF study. Preventive automatic response follows weighted requests clipped to each participant's effective active bounds and optional movement limits. A common response signal lets unsaturated generators take over unmet response. Default weights use nonzero effective active capability ranges; explicit weights override them. Zero-weight generators hold their base output. The signal is power in p.u., not frequency, and differs from total delivered response after saturation. Corrective response permits abs(P[c,g] - P[base,g]) <= redispatch_limits[g]. Missing limits mean zero redispatch. Limits also optionally cap preventive response. All powers use the case per-unit base. Generator capability limits apply in every scenario. Outaged generators have zero P/Q and no active droop equation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.Study"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.TapControl","page":"API","text":"Explicit continuous tap policy. Omitted branches remain fixed at supplied ratios.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.TapControl"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#DroopOPF.VoltVarDroop","page":"API","text":"A static volt-var droop with exact PWL semantics and constant Q capability.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.VoltVarDroop"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF._branch_admittances-Tuple{Branch}","page":"API","text":"Two-terminal admittance primitive for a fixed, from-side complex tap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF._branch_admittances"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF._build_complementarity_opf_model-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"Build an exact PWL-droop AC OPF model for CCOpt.\n\nEach voltage hinge is represented as z = 0  z - a = 0. Reactive clipping is the KKT system of projection of the raw droop value onto the reactive capability interval. Positive-width deadbands use four pairs per attachment. Constant zero-width deadbands use the affine raw response directly and only the two reactive projection pairs, avoiding redundant voltage hinges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF._build_complementarity_opf_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF._matpower_array-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"Minimal MATPOWER v2 reader for balanced AC network data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF._matpower_array"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.assemble_scopf_response_stages-Tuple{Study, Study, SCOPFResult, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"assemble_scopf_response_stages(immediate_study, settled_study, immediate, settled; ...)\n\nAssemble and independently validate a paired response-stage assessment from already solved results. This is the resumable/process-isolated counterpart to assess_scopf_response_stages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.assemble_scopf_response_stages"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.assess_contingency_pipeline-Tuple{Case, ACState}","page":"API","text":"assess_contingency_pipeline(case, base_state; ...)\n\nRun a coverage-checked M10 assessment pipeline. Every catalog entry receives exactly one route. audit_screened_ids names screened entries that must also receive exact evaluation. Set screening_enabled=false for an exact connected- outage baseline. Evaluation errors are retained unless continue_on_error=false. workers=1 preserves serial execution. Larger values use bounded threaded batches with independent models, serial native solves, and deterministic coordinator callbacks/checkpoints. Start Julia with enough threads. Phase timings sum worker durations and are not parallel wall time. User model hooks, if supplied, must be thread-safe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.assess_contingency_pipeline"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.assess_scopf_response_stages-Tuple{Study, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"assess_scopf_response_stages(immediate_study, settled_study, base_result; ...)\n\nHold the validated base state and equipment settings fixed, then solve the same contingencies twice: first with preventive equipment frozen and then with the declared settled policy. These are paired equilibria, not a transient simulation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.assess_scopf_response_stages"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.attach_controls-Tuple{Case, AbstractVector{<:VoltVarDroop}, AbstractVector{<:GeneratorControlAttachment}}","page":"API","text":"Return a copy of case with the supplied M1 generator controls attached.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.attach_controls"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.bank_admittance-Union{Tuple{ShuntBank{T}}, Tuple{T}} where T","page":"API","text":"Aggregate admittance at the supplied bank state; zero when unavailable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.bank_admittance"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.bank_powers-Tuple{ACNetwork, ACState}","page":"API","text":"Bank terminal consumption in network.banks order, independently summed per step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.bank_powers"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.benchmark_scopf-Tuple{Study}","page":"API","text":"benchmark_scopf(study; samples=3, warmup=true, ...)\n\nMeasure complete model construction, optimization, and result extraction. The optional warm-up solve is excluded. Allocation bytes are Julia allocations, not process peak RSS. process_peak_rss_bytes separately records the operating system's lifetime process high-water mark, including compilation, warm-up, solver native memory, and validation. It is not a per-solve increment. Run different studies in fresh processes for comparable peaks. Model size is counted from the actual JuMP model passed to the optimizer. Solver keywords are forwarded to solve_scopf.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.benchmark_scopf"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.best_droop_slope-Tuple{DroopSlopeSweep}","page":"API","text":"Return the valid sweep point with the smallest M2 dispatch objective.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.best_droop_slope"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.build_global_feasibility_model-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem}","page":"API","text":"Build a bounded voltage-restoration model for a frozen fixed-equipment problem.\n\nmode=:global uses rectangular AC voltages and exact SOS2 droop regions. mode=:soc uses sparse edge-product SOC envelopes and droop graph convex hulls. Only target_bus may exceed its original upper voltage bound, by delta in [0,max_relaxation]. Frozen feasibility tolerances are included in both models. The SOC model is an outer relaxation, never a feasible-point certificate. Angle windows outside (-pi/2,pi/2) are explicitly omitted (an outer relaxation). Pass an optional global/conic solver factory; no optimization occurs here.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.build_global_feasibility_model"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ccopt_diagnostics-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"API","text":"Read final CCOpt/MadNLP relaxation diagnostics from a solved JuMP model.\n\nThe iteration count is the cumulative inner MadNLP count exposed by CCOpt. The package does not currently expose a separate outer-homotopy iteration count. These are final relaxed-NLP metrics, not an original-MPCC stationarity proof. When CCOpt delegates a model without complementarity constraints to MadNLP, the pair count is zero and relaxation and CCOpt-only timing fields are nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ccopt_diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.ccopt_encoding_audit-Tuple{JuMP.Model}","page":"API","text":"Compare CCOpt's solved auxiliary MPCC variables with direct exact-droop evaluation.\n\nThis is a read-only diagnostic for distinguishing a relaxed-complementarity iterate from an extraction/model-bridge discrepancy. It does not change the model, stopping rules, or physical acceptance criteria.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.ccopt_encoding_audit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.certify_feasibility_lp-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem}","page":"API","text":"Try a sparse outer LP and return only independently verified infeasibility.\n\nThe optional LP solver supplies candidate Farkas multipliers. The checker uses exact rational arithmetic, including residual stationarity errors over finite variable bounds. SOC/thermal tangent cuts are independently validated. Optional psd_clique_limit (0 disables; 3–32 enables) adds bounded-size cycle PSD support cuts, whose rational coefficients are reconstructed independently. Feasible LP points never qualify the original nonconvex problem. The work is bounded by time_limit and max_rounds; no global solver is required by the main package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.certify_feasibility_lp"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.check_feasibility-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem}","page":"API","text":"Replay seeds, check global necessary conditions, then optionally search locally.\n\nA local failure, positive local restoration objective, or absence of a witness returns :unresolved. :certified_infeasible requires an independently replayed certificate. Optional lp_options and global_options select bounded escalation backends; neither runs by default. Solver-reported global bounds alone do not upgrade the gate. Inconclusive certificates or unsupported backends are unresolved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.check_feasibility"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.check_psse_file-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"Check a RAW saved power-flow solution and optionally save a JSON report.\n\nThis check-only path never exposes a case with invented thermal ratings. It uses an internal finite adapter placeholder for zero/omitted ratings solely to replay AC physics, excludes those placeholders from every thermal check, and reports incomplete thermal coverage. Ordinary load_psse_case still requires an explicit unrated_limit before it can return such a case. Unsupported data return status=\"unsupported\"; malformed input returns status=\"invalid_file\".\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.check_psse_file"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.contingency_catalog-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"contingency_catalog(case; include_branches=true, include_generators=true)\n\nEnumerate every available single branch and generator outage without silently discarding candidates. Branch outages that island the network are retained as :excluded with reason :islanding_unsupported, because the current SCOPF formulation has one reference angle and does not support island balancing. Generator outages and connected line/transformer outages are :eligible.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.contingency_catalog"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.contingency_screening_scores-Tuple{Case, ACState}","page":"API","text":"contingency_screening_scores(case, state; catalog=contingency_catalog(case))\n\nPrioritize eligible outages using base-state branch loading within the branch family and active-power share within the generator family. Scores are empirical within-family percentiles so unlike raw quantities can share one queue. This is a screening feature only: low score never means safe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.contingency_screening_scores"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.default_participation-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"default_participation(case; reactive_assignments=nothing)\n\nCapability-range weights for available generators with nonzero effective active range. These are a declared fallback, not measured governor/AGC response data. Supply explicit nonnegative weights when actual response data are available.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.default_participation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.equilibrium_report-Tuple{Case, ACOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Combine solver metadata with an independent equilibrium validation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.equilibrium_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.evaluate_contingencies-Tuple{Study, ACState}","page":"API","text":"Evaluate all contingencies while holding the supplied base equilibrium fixed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.evaluate_contingencies"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.evaluate_contingency_catalog-Tuple{Case, ACState}","page":"API","text":"evaluate_contingency_catalog(case, base_state; catalog, participation, kwargs...)\n\nEvaluate every structurally eligible catalog entry as an independent fixed-base problem. The base state must independently validate. Excluded entries remain in the catalog and are never silently treated as safe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.evaluate_contingency_catalog"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.evaluate_held_out_contingencies-Tuple{Study, DroopDesignResult, AbstractVector{<:Contingency}}","page":"API","text":"Evaluate an optimized base dispatch against contingencies excluded from training.\n\nReturns a named tuple containing the held-out study, result, and independent report. Held-out IDs must differ from the training scenario IDs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.evaluate_held_out_contingencies"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.evaluate_islanding_contingency-Tuple{Case, Contingency, ACState}","page":"API","text":"Solve and independently validate the retained component of an islanding outage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.evaluate_islanding_contingency"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.exact_droop_audit-Tuple{Case, JointDesignResult}","page":"API","text":"Independently audit each controlled generator against its exact physical droop curve.\n\nFor a complementarity result this is an extraction check, not a second solve: it compares returned qg values with direct evaluation of the exact PWL curve after applying any optimized tap, shunt, and droop settings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.exact_droop_audit"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.feasibility_fingerprint-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem}","page":"API","text":"Return the scope SHA-256, rejecting mutation of any frozen physical data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.feasibility_fingerprint"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.filter_contingencies-Tuple{Case, ACState}","page":"API","text":"filter_contingencies(case, base_state; loading_threshold=0.8, voltage_guard=0.02)\n\nRoute N-1 candidates before exact AC evaluation. Generator outages always go to exact evaluation. Islanding branches go to the island-aware path. Connected line and transformer outages use a lossless DC LODF prediction; numerical uncertainty and a tight base-voltage margin fall back to exact evaluation. reactive_guard=0.1 escalates when aggregate bidirectional Q headroom is at most 10% of available Q range. ac_risk_guard=true also checks apparent-flow redistribution and an impedance-based incremental voltage-drop indicator. These are escalation heuristics, not AC power-flow solutions or bounds. Set reactive_guard=0, ac_risk_guard=false to replay the legacy policy. Opt-in droop_response_guard=true adds individual Q-reserve and bounded AC/droop-response risk checks for fixed-equipment legacy droops. Unsupported or uncertain predictions escalate to exact AC; the default remains unchanged. participation must match the subsequent fixed-base exact evaluation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.filter_contingencies"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.import_psse_case-Tuple{PSSERawData}","page":"API","text":"Convert the representable AC subset of parsed RAW data without dropping devices. Supplied tap ratios/phases and shunt BINIT remain fixed; automatic regulation is retained as RAW metadata, not inferred as droop or AVR. ZIP current loads, DC, FACTS, node/breaker topology, three-winding transformers and magnetizing models are rejected. Zero thermal ratings require an explicit unrated_limit in pu. The returned source maps preserve bus/ID and I/J/CKT identities; parsed operating points are retained separately from clamped generator initialization hints.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.import_psse_case"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.islanded_scenario_case-Tuple{Case, Contingency, ACState}","page":"API","text":"islanded_scenario_case(case, contingency, base_state)\n\nBuild the connected component to balance after a branch outage. The original reference-bus component is retained unless another component has strictly more buses. In that case the largest component is retained and the bus of its largest available generator becomes the new reference. Ties retain the original reference component. Discarded load and generation are reported.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.islanded_scenario_case"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.load_matpower_case-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"load_matpower_case(path; base_frequency = 50.0, id = nothing)\n\nLoad the core bus, generator, load, and branch tables from a MATPOWER v2 case file. Values are normalized to the case base power. TAP=0 maps to unity; SHIFT is converted from degrees to radians. Nonzero bus GS/BS become aggregate fixed shunts with G=GS/baseMVA and B=BS/baseMVA. Other unsupported fields (including costs and angle-difference limits) remain outside this adapter. Fixed ratios and phase shifts are supported by AC evaluation and OPF/SCOPF.\n\nRATE_A == 0 is treated as an unavailable thermal limit and represented by a large finite limit so that the M1 model remains numerically well-defined.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.load_matpower_case"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.load_psse_case-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"Load the strict supported PSS/E RAW AC subset as a Case; use import_psse_case(parse_psse_raw(path)) for source identities and diagnostics. Unrated branches require an explicit unrated_limit (pu). Saved-state checking runs automatically and warns on failure; require_valid=true rejects a failed check. report_path optionally writes JSON. Use validation_options=(;...) for tolerances, or explicit check_saved_solution=false to disable checking. For report-only checks of unrated/unsupported files, use check_psse_file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.load_psse_case"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.markdown_report-Tuple{DroopDesignMultiStartResult}","page":"API","text":"Render M3 parameter multi-start results as Markdown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.markdown_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.markdown_report-Tuple{DroopSecurityResult}","page":"API","text":"Summarize contingency addition without converting unresolved cases into passes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.markdown_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.markdown_report-Tuple{EquilibriumReport}","page":"API","text":"Render an equilibrium report as compact Markdown for logs and artifacts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.markdown_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.markdown_report-Tuple{SCOPFDiagnostics}","page":"API","text":"Render structured findings and breakpoint distances as Markdown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.markdown_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.markdown_report-Tuple{SCOPFMultiStartResult}","page":"API","text":"Render every multi-start outcome and the comparison decision as Markdown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.markdown_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.markdown_report-Tuple{SCOPFReport}","page":"API","text":"Render SCOPF status, tolerances, exact-curve checks, and scenario failures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.markdown_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.mixed_reactive_controls-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"Assign AVR to the largest fraction of online positive-Pmax generators.\n\nRound half upward by count; ties use ascending generator ID. ratings must explicitly supply positive system-base apparent ratings for every eligible unit. voltage_targets must supply an AVR target for each selected generator. Remaining online units retain their attached volt-var curves; offline records remain offline and receive FreeQ (no active control equation).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.mixed_reactive_controls"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_droop_multistart-Tuple{Study, Integer, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"optimize_droop_multistart(study, control_id, starts; ...)\n\nOptimize the same bounded M3 design from every named DroopSettings start. All attempts and exact-curve validation reports are retained. When omitted, reference_result is solved once and shared by every design run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_droop_multistart"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_droop_parameters-Tuple{Study, AbstractVector{<:DroopControl}}","page":"API","text":"Optimize several shared droop curves using the normal smooth SCOPF builder.\n\nThe Study equipment and active-response policies are preserved. Explicit mixed assignments may design matching attached droop controls. Initial states are numerical starts only; independently replay the returned exact curves.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_droop_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_droop_parameters-Tuple{Study, Integer}","page":"API","text":"optimize_droop_parameters(study, control_id; slope_bounds, ...)\n\nOptimize one control's slope, voltage reference, and asymmetric deadband widths as variables shared by the base case and every training contingency. Omitted bounds fix that setting at its M2 value. The dispatch objective is unchanged, so fixing all bounds at the reference reproduces M2. This is a smooth-NLP design problem for Ipopt or MadNLP; CCOpt may be used only after reconstructing the optimized fixed curve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_droop_parameters"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_droop_security-Tuple{Study, AbstractVector{<:DroopControl}}","page":"API","text":"optimize_droop_security(study, controls; catalog=contingency_catalog(study.case), ...)\n\nIteratively add unresolved N-1 outages to shared droop design. Study contingencies are the initial training set and must use catalog identities. Every successful termination requires independent full AC assessment of all eligible catalog entries at the final settings/base. Excluded islands and catalog subsets cannot produce fulln1accepted. Errors, rejected masters and round limits are retained. The callback receives a complete immutable round after its decisions are made.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_droop_security"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_joint_design-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"Optimize joint base-case controls; control_normalization=:bounds optionally uses unit-interval affine coordinates for free settings. droop_q_bounds=:implied omits generator-Q bounds already implied in exact arithmetic by the smoothed droop saturation and nested control capability. droop_q_formulation=:reduced substitutes that response into balance and objective expressions, eliminating the controlled-generator Q variable and droop equality. These options preserve the physical equations, objective and returned settings; their defaults preserve the original formulation. smooth_reactive_epsilon optionally fixes the absolute reactive smoothing width across controllers; nothing retains capability-scaled relative smoothing. It has no effect on the exact complementarity encoding.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_joint_design"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_multi_period-Tuple{MultiPeriodStudy}","page":"API","text":"Locally minimize duration-weighted active energy loss, with shared droop design and per-period AC dispatch coupled by explicit generator ramp rates. Equipment remains supplied and fixed in all periods. Uses sparse original-network AC.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_multi_period"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_multi_period_security-Tuple{Any}","page":"API","text":"Optimize shared droop across ordered periods and listed contingencies.\n\nresponse=:s2_proportional preserves supplied weights without saturation; :limit_aware uses the production response model. s2_emergency=true explicitly selects S2's ±0.05 pu voltage extension, 1.1 thermal ratings, 1% per-load DR, 10 MW shedding per outage, 10% combined relief, proportional reactive relief. Base cases always use original bounds/full load. Objectives are sequential: minimum total shedding, minimum total DR, then duration-weighted base loss. objective_policy=:reserve_loss inserts maximization of the minimum participating-generator upward-headroom fraction before loss; :reserve stops after the reserve stage. This is a dispatch preference, not an outage-security certificate. Equipment follows each Study policy; ramps link preventive dispatch only. Optional avr_bounds schedules preventive targets with movement in pu/hour; otherwise all supplied targets remain fixed. Optional reserve_generator_outages requires surviving participation to have upward room for each named generator's entire base output plus reserve_margin_pu, before any load relief. This explicit reserve target does not account for changed losses or replace exact outage AC. Retain this extra policy externally with the input studies for independent audit. objective_policy=:feasibility seeks a constrained witness without resource or loss optimization. Each initial-equipment entry may carry a scenarios dictionary of per-scenario taps/shunts starting values, falling back to its period entry. These starts do not change equipment bounds, timing, movement budgets or shared constraints. initial_avr_targets, initial_relief and initial_balancing_power retain the remaining primal variables across retries; each supplies one dictionary per period. Unknown scenario/target IDs, nonfinite values and inconsistent shared targets are rejected. AVR and individual relief starts are clipped to variable bounds. No manufacturer timing is inferred.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_multi_period_security"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_shunts-Tuple{Case, AbstractVector{ShuntControl}}","page":"API","text":"Optimize simple capacitor/reactor banks in base-case OPF; taps and droops stay fixed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_shunts"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.optimize_taps-Tuple{Case, AbstractVector{TapControl}}","page":"API","text":"Base-case AC OPF with explicitly selected continuous tap ratios.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.optimize_taps"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.parse_psse_raw-Tuple{IO}","page":"API","text":"Parse full-case PSS/E bus/branch RAW revisions 33, 34 and 36 from a path or IO. Known core records have typed fields; unmodeled sections remain opaque and intact. Errors name the source line. Omitted/empty optional fields remain absent, while text preserves comments, unknown fields, quoted identifiers and original units. Set encoding=:latin1 for legacy extended-ASCII files (default :utf8).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.parse_psse_raw"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.q_ref-Tuple{VoltVarDroop}","page":"API","text":"Preferred name for the reactive-power reference inside the voltage deadband.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.q_ref"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.qualify_droop_replacement-Tuple{Case, Case}","page":"API","text":"Qualify a proposed droop replacement before accepting it for S1 analysis.\n\nThe source calibration point must replay both before and after replacement; optimization cannot repair a failed calibration check. Then both the original and replacement cases must have a witness at every required demand factor. All candidate curves are tested together. This does not certify shared SCOPF dispatch or preventive equipment settings across scenarios.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.qualify_droop_replacement"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.rank_contingencies-Tuple{ContingencyCatalog, Any}","page":"API","text":"rank_contingencies(catalog, report)\n\nRank the contingencies represented in an independently generated SCOPFReport. The ranking is an evaluation result, not a pre-solve filter: invalid and physical-only results remain visible and sort ahead of accepted results. Among accepted results, lower voltage, generator, or thermal margin means higher severity. A catalog entry absent from report is omitted rather than presumed safe.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.rank_contingencies"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.reactive_control_assignments-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"Project a legacy droop-only case into one explicit mode per generator.\n\nGenerators without a legacy attachment become FreeQ; existing attachments become VoltVarDroop assignments. The case itself is not modified.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.reactive_control_assignments"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.reactive_limits-Tuple{Generator, Real}","page":"API","text":"Effective generator reactive interval at active output p, in system-base pu. The circle supplements, rather than replaces, the supplied rectangular bounds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.reactive_limits"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.reactive_smoothing_epsilon-Tuple{VoltVarDroop, Real}","page":"API","text":"Return the reactive-power smoothing width for one droop control.\n\nThe relative default is expressed as a fraction of the smaller distance from the deadband reactive reference to either capability limit. This makes the regularization comparable for generators with different ratings and power bases.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.reactive_smoothing_epsilon"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.read_droop_design-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"Read an M3 design; independently validate it against its study before use.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.read_droop_design"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.read_droop_slope_sweep-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"Read a droop-slope sweep written by write_droop_slope_sweep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.read_droop_slope_sweep"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.read_feasibility_result-Tuple{Any, FeasibilityProblem}","page":"API","text":"Load cached evidence only for an explicitly supplied matching problem, and replay it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.read_feasibility_result"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.read_scopf_result-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"Load saved states and metadata. Revalidate them against the corresponding study before use.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.read_scopf_result"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.read_study-Tuple{AbstractString}","page":"API","text":"Load a study and recheck case, outage, and response-policy validity.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.read_study"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.require_qualified_replacement-Tuple{DroopQualification}","page":"API","text":"Return the candidate only after every required condition has a revalidated witness.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.require_qualified_replacement"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.run_contingency_campaign-Tuple{AbstractString, Case, ACState}","page":"API","text":"run_contingency_campaign(directory, case, base_state; ...)\n\nRun the integrated pipeline with atomic per-outage JSON checkpoints. Resume requires identical case, catalog, base state, participation, screening/audit policy, solver settings, Julia version, source and dependency environment. Mismatched contracts are rejected without overwriting prior evidence. Corrupt checkpoints and evaluation errors are rerun; saved states are independently revalidated. A directory lock prevents concurrent writers. on_checkpoint is a progress callback after durable writes (and can interrupt the campaign). Returns (result, resumed_count, evaluated_count, fingerprint).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.run_contingency_campaign"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.scenario_case-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{Case{T}, Contingency}} where T","page":"API","text":"Apply outages without changing the case, device order, or control settings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.scenario_case"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.scopf_diagnostics-Tuple{Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"scopf_diagnostics(study, result; breakpoint_tolerance=1e-3, binding_tolerance=1e-4, ...)\n\nIndependently validate a result, measure every active control's nearest-breakpoint distance, identify device-level binding limits, and select the contingency with the smallest independently recomputed physical margin. The critical-scenario score is a screening heuristic, not a dynamic stability ranking.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.scopf_diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.scopf_operating_points-Tuple{Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Return all available-generator droop operating points grouped by scenario.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.scopf_operating_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.seed_global_feasibility!-Tuple{Any, ACState}","page":"API","text":"Map an AC point to every lifted variable and SOS2 weight, without changing the problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.seed_global_feasibility!"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.shunt_powers-Tuple{ACNetwork, ACState}","page":"API","text":"Complex consumption at each fixed shunt, in network.shunts order. Capacitors have negative reactive consumption. Unavailable shunts return zero. This evaluator uses terminal voltage/current, independently of optimizer Ybus.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.shunt_powers"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.solve_global_feasibility-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem}","page":"API","text":"Run one explicitly budgeted global/SOC escalation and return its gate result and ledger.\n\nOnly independently replayed AC points can upgrade the gate here. Floating-point solver bounds are retained as solver_reported_infeasibility evidence, not promoted to independently verified certificates. This distinction also holds at time limits and when the solver reports OPTIMAL/INFEASIBLE.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solve_global_feasibility"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.solve_opf_complementarity-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"Solve an AC OPF with exact complementarity-encoded volt-var controls.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solve_opf_complementarity"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.solve_opf_continuation-Tuple{Case}","page":"API","text":"solve_opf_continuation(case; smooth_epsilons, initial_state, kwargs...)\n\nSolve a sequence of smoothed OPF problems, passing each available state as the warm start for the next epsilon. The supplied epsilon sequence should usually decrease from a robust, relatively smooth value to the desired final value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solve_opf_continuation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.solve_scopf-Tuple{Study}","page":"API","text":"solve_scopf(study; smooth_epsilon=1e-4, optimizer_factory=Ipopt.Optimizer, ...)\n\nSolve the base and all listed contingencies jointly, minimizing the M1 base schedule-deviation objective. Contingencies impose feasibility constraints and have no additional cost. The fixed droop graph applies in every available unit. fixed_base_state evaluates security of an existing equilibrium without changing it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solve_scopf"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.solve_scopf_continuation-Tuple{Study}","page":"API","text":"Solve successive smoothing widths, warm-starting all scenarios; stop on solver failure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solve_scopf_continuation"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.solve_scopf_multistart-Tuple{Study, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"solve_scopf_multistart(study, starts; objective_atol=1e-7, objective_rtol=1e-6, ...)\n\nSolve and independently validate every named initial-state dictionary. A start may be empty for a cold solve. All runs are retained. Valid objectives are classified as :comparable, :divergent, :insufficient_valid_runs, or :no_valid_runs; the best run is identified only among independently valid results.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solve_scopf_multistart"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.solver_operating_points-Tuple{Case, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"Return droop operating points grouped with the solver that produced them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.solver_operating_points"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.sweep_droop_slope-Tuple{Study, Integer, Any}","page":"API","text":"Evaluate bounded fixed-slope M2 studies, anchored and warm-started at a reference slope.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.sweep_droop_slope"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.tap_design_metrics-Tuple{Case, TapOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Raw physical and setting metrics; none implicitly adds an objective term.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.tap_design_metrics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_contingency_filter-Tuple{Case, ACState}","page":"API","text":"validate_contingency_filter(case, base_state; ...)\n\nCompare screening with exhaustive AC routes on the same catalog and base. Returns per-device-family counts of screened unresolved results, confirmed physical failures, and recall of unresolved outcomes at each ranking budget. An unresolved result includes solver rejection/error and is not a proof of infeasibility. Zero observed misses is evidence only for this input set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_contingency_filter"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_droop_design-Tuple{Study, DroopDesignResult}","page":"API","text":"Independently replay an optimized design with an exact physical droop curve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_droop_design"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_droop_design-Tuple{Study, SCOPFDroopDesignResult}","page":"API","text":"Check shared parameter bounds and independently replay exact SCOPF physics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_droop_design"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_equilibrium-Tuple{Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"validate_equilibrium(study, result; coupling_tolerance=1e-6, ...)\n\nRebuild each outage independently and check AC physics, exact droop replay, availability, and coupling to the base dispatch. Missing scenarios and states produce invalid reports. physical_valid is separate from solver success.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_equilibrium"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_feasibility_witness-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem, FeasibilityWitness}","page":"API","text":"Independently replay a witness against its frozen scope. Optimizer status is irrelevant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_feasibility_witness"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_multi_period-Tuple{MultiPeriodStudy, MultiPeriodResult}","page":"API","text":"Independent original-network, exact-droop, shared-design, ramp, angle and energy-objective replay. Native convergence is required separately from physics.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_multi_period"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_multi_period_security-Tuple{Any, MultiPeriodSecurityResult}","page":"API","text":"Replay original AC physics, exact mixed controls, circles, response, relief, equipment bounds/timing, preventive ramps, angles and objective without solving.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_multi_period_security"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_psse_saved_solution-Tuple{PSSEImport}","page":"API","text":"Validate the voltages, angles and generator P/Q saved in RAW, without solving.\n\nReturns a JSON-compatible report separating AC balance, known operating limits, and missing thermal ratings. Default balance thresholds are 0.01 MW/MVAr per bus, explicitly allowing ordinary RAW export rounding; core equilibrium validation tolerances are unchanged. A pass covers the imported AC subset, not PSS/E convergence, automatic controls, dynamics or external branch-flow data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_psse_saved_solution"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_reactive_assignments-Tuple{Case, AbstractVector{<:ReactiveControlAssignment}}","page":"API","text":"Validate explicit reactive-control assignments against one case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_reactive_assignments"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.validate_tap_design-Tuple{Case, TapOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Separately report numerical termination, physical validity and tap-policy compliance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.validate_tap_design"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.verified_feasibility_voltage_bounds-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem, FeasibilityResult}","page":"API","text":"Replay every LP weak-duality bound proof and return outward-rounded voltage bounds.\n\nThis never trusts the ledger's displayed bounds. Bounds from the original strict problem must not be reused as bounds on a voltage-relaxed problem.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.verified_feasibility_voltage_bounds"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.verify_feasibility_result-Tuple{FeasibilityProblem, FeasibilityResult}","page":"API","text":"Revalidate evidence and scope instead of trusting a stored status or fingerprint alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.verify_feasibility_result"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.with_bank_state-Union{Tuple{T}, Tuple{ShuntBank{T}, Any}} where T","page":"API","text":"Copy a bank with a supplied legal state/availability, leaving its design intact.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.with_bank_state"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.with_droop_settings-Tuple{Study, Integer, DroopSettings}","page":"API","text":"Return a copy of a study with one control replaced by optimized settings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.with_droop_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.with_droop_settings-Tuple{Study, SCOPFDroopDesignResult}","page":"API","text":"Reconstruct all shared curves while retaining the complete Study policy.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.with_droop_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.with_joint_settings-Tuple{Case, JointDesignResult}","page":"API","text":"Reconstruct all physical settings for independent joint-design replay.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.with_joint_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.with_shunt_settings-Tuple{Case, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"Physical replay of relaxed banks as equivalent fixed admittances; original bank metadata remain in the input case.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.with_shunt_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.with_tap_settings-Tuple{Case, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"Reconstruct physical data at explicitly supplied ratios without changing the input.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.with_tap_settings"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_contingency_catalog-Tuple{AbstractString, ContingencyCatalog}","page":"API","text":"Write a structurally classified N-1 candidate catalog to JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_contingency_catalog"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_contingency_pipeline-Tuple{AbstractString, ContingencyPipelineResult}","page":"API","text":"Write the complete routed contingency pipeline and its checks to JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_contingency_pipeline"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_contingency_ranking-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractVector{<:ContingencyRank}}","page":"API","text":"Write an exact-evaluation contingency ranking to JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_contingency_ranking"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_design-Tuple{AbstractString, DroopDesignResult}","page":"API","text":"Write an optimized M3 droop design and its training result to versioned JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_design"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_design_comparison_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, DroopDesignResult}","page":"API","text":"Write the reference and optimized exact droop curves with validation points.\n\nTraining operating points come from design.result. When both held-out keywords are supplied, non-base held-out points are added with a distinct marker colour. The input study must contain the reference settings used to create design.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_design_comparison_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_design_multistart-Tuple{AbstractString, DroopDesignMultiStartResult}","page":"API","text":"Write M3 parameter-start inputs, outcomes, and spreads to versioned JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_design_multistart"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_plot","page":"API","text":"Write exact volt-var curves and operating points to a self-contained SVG.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_qualification-Tuple{Any, DroopQualification}","page":"API","text":"Save the complete precursor qualification, including blocked conditions.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_qualification"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_security-Tuple{AbstractString, Union{DroopSecurityResult, SCOPFDroopDesignResult}}","page":"API","text":"Persist shared droop settings, complete iteration evidence and final coverage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_security"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_slope_sweep-Tuple{AbstractString, DroopSlopeSweep}","page":"API","text":"Write a versioned, machine-readable droop-slope sweep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_slope_sweep"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_droop_slope_sweep_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, DroopSlopeSweep}","page":"API","text":"Write a three-panel SVG of objective, voltage deviation, and branch loading.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_droop_slope_sweep_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_feasibility_result-Tuple{Any, FeasibilityProblem, FeasibilityResult}","page":"API","text":"Save the full frozen problem, independently checked evidence and search attempts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_feasibility_result"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_psse_validation_report-Tuple{AbstractString, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"Write a local JSON report. Nonfinite diagnostic values become JSON null.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_psse_validation_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_benchmark-Tuple{AbstractString, SCOPFBenchmarkReport}","page":"API","text":"Write reproducible SCOPF benchmark metadata and samples to versioned JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_benchmark"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_branch_loading_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write branch loading percentages, marking unavailable branches as outages.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_branch_loading_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_diagnostics-Tuple{AbstractString, SCOPFDiagnostics}","page":"API","text":"Write breakpoint distances and structured findings to versioned JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_diagnostics"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_dispatch_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write active and reactive generator dispatch for every scenario.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_dispatch_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_droop_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write exact droop curves with base and contingency operating points.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_droop_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_evaluation_report-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write a Markdown index connecting SCOPF inputs, outputs, plots, and validation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_evaluation_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_input_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study}","page":"API","text":"Plot declared outage, response, and equipment-policy inputs by scenario.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_input_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_margin_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Plot independently recomputed operating margins for every SCOPF scenario.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_margin_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_multistart-Tuple{AbstractString, SCOPFMultiStartResult}","page":"API","text":"Write all multi-start outcomes and the objective-comparison classification.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_multistart"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_network_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study}","page":"API","text":"Write a topology-and-equipment diagram for the network declared in a SCOPF study.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_network_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_report-Tuple{AbstractString, SCOPFReport}","page":"API","text":"Write a report to JSON; null margins mean an absent bound and null residuals are unavailable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_report"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_residual_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write residual-to-tolerance ratios for AC balance, exact droop, and coupling.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_residual_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_response_delta_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Plot maximum base-to-scenario state changes on a logarithmic scale.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_response_delta_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_result-Tuple{AbstractString, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write scenario states and solver metadata to JSON. Nonfinite diagnostics become null.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_result"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_stage_assessment-Tuple{AbstractString, SCOPFStageAssessment}","page":"API","text":"Write a paired M9.3 immediate/settled assessment to JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_stage_assessment"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_validation_matrix_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Plot independent scenario checks, including invalid and corrupted results.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_validation_matrix_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_validation_plots-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write the complete SCOPF input/output validation bundle and return its file paths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_validation_plots"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_scopf_voltage_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Study, SCOPFResult}","page":"API","text":"Write bus-voltage trajectories and their limits for every scenario.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_scopf_voltage_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_solver_comparison_plot-Tuple{AbstractString, Case, AbstractDict}","page":"API","text":"Write exact droop curves with operating points from several solvers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_solver_comparison_plot"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#DroopOPF.write_study-Tuple{AbstractString, Study}","page":"API","text":"Write a versioned study including its case and response policy to JSON.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DroopOPF.write_study"},{"category":"section","location":"mixed_avr_droop/#Mixed-AVR-and-volt–var-droop-studies","page":"Mixed AVR / droop","text":"Historical artifacts/ paths on this page identify local campaign records, not files bundled with the release. See archive access and the current qualification index.\n\nGenerator(...; s_rated=S) declares a positive apparent-power rating in per-unit on the case system MVA base. Omission (nothing) preserves the historical rectangular capability. An explicit rating adds P²+Q² ≤ S² in each optimized AC state; it never replaces P/Q bounds. The setting survives Case conversion, contingencies, retained islands and study JSON (rated studies use schema 11). PSS/E MBASE is not automatically interpreted as a verified nameplate rating. For capability-clipped droop and individual AVR controls with Q intervals spanning zero, the clipped response plus |P|≤S already enforces the circle algebraically. The builder omits a duplicate circle row in those cases to avoid a dependent saturation Jacobian. Other units retain explicit circle inequalities. Independent replay checks the circle for every rated unit regardless of numerical encoding.\n\nreactive_limits(g,p) returns the intersection of the supplied Q interval and [-sqrt(S²-p²), sqrt(S²-p²)]. AVR releases voltage regulation at these effective limits. A volt–var curve retains its designed reference, slope and deadband, but its delivered response clips to the effective capability. Several AVR units regulating the same location share the same normalized position in their currently available reactive intervals. Their target must be consistent.\n\nThis is a steady-state control and capability model. The labels AVR and droop do not instantiate synchronous-machine dynamics, a grid-following PLL, a grid-forming controller, current priority or renewable availability. Detailed inverter capabilities remain P3 work. The circle is an apparent-power limit; it does not impose the voltage-dependent current envelope of a real converter.","title":"Mixed AVR and volt–var droop studies"},{"category":"section","location":"mixed_avr_droop/#Deterministic-allocation","page":"Mixed AVR / droop","text":"mixed_reactive_controls(case; avr_fraction=.30, ratings, voltage_targets) ranks online, positive-Pmax units by descending Pmax and ascending ID. The AVR count rounds to nearest integer, half upward. Remaining online units retain their supplied attached volt–var curves. Offline records stay offline. The caller must provide ratings for every eligible unit and targets for exactly the selected AVR units. No device rating is inferred by this helper.\n\nExplicit mixed assignments can be combined with smooth free droop design in optimize_joint_design, optimize_droop_parameters and optimize_multi_period. Stable control IDs refer to legacy attachments; a selected free curve must match the explicit assignment for that generator. Exact complementarity supports fixed mixed curves and P-dependent saturation; free mixed-curve design currently uses the smooth formulation. The frozen feasibility certificate gate (including rational LP and global graph escalation) explicitly rejects rated cases until its P-dependent graph is extended. This avoids applying an obsolete voltage-only droop hull to clipped capability. Ordinary AC/SCOPF native solves and their physical replay support the ratings. The optional legacy voltage-only droop predictor also returns an unsupported guard for rated cases and routes them to exact assessment.","title":"Deterministic allocation"},{"category":"section","location":"mixed_avr_droop/#Multi-period-security","page":"Mixed AVR / droop","text":"optimize_multi_period_security(studies; duration_hours, ramp_limits, angle_limits, droop_controls, ...) couples preventive active dispatch by supplied pu/hour ramp rates and uses one shared designed curve per selected controller. Each period has its own base and declared outage states. Equipment follows the Study selections, bounds, timing and movement budgets. Unselected equipment stays fixed. No initial/terminal dispatch, outage trajectories, storage or commitment are inferred. Normal base bounds and full demand always apply.\n\nresponse=:limit_aware uses the production saturating participation model.\nresponse=:s2_proportional explicitly uses the saved S2 proportional equality. It does not silently turn into a saturated response.\ns2_emergency=true selects the specific S2 comparison policy: outage voltage bounds extended by 0.05 pu, branch MVA limits multiplied by 1.1, DR up to 1% of each positive load, shedding up to 10 MW per outage, combined relief up to 10% per load, and proportional reactive relief. These are declared assumptions, not universal security defaults or transient guarantees.\nEmergency solves sequentially minimize total shedding, total DR, then duration-weighted base loss energy. Earlier optima are retained within 1e-8 system-base pu in the aggregate objective. These remain local NLP stages.\nAVR targets are fixed by default. Optional avr_bounds=Dict(id=>(lo,hi)) schedules a preventive target per period, shared across its contingencies; avr_movement_per_hour bounds changes between period starts. This parameter is an explicit study assumption, not a manufacturer ramp specification. Units at one regulated location share one exact target, using intersected bounds. An unscheduled AVR at that location holds the common target fixed.\nFixed-base evaluation freezes the common curves, states and equipment, then solves listed outage recourse with a feasibility objective; it does not minimize the relief of each accepted response. It is a security assessment of a candidate, not a replacement for jointly optimizing all required constraints.\n\nAccept only native success and validate_multi_period_security(studies,r).valid. Replay independently reconstructs equipment, load relief, original-network currents, exact controls, apparent limits, response, angles, temporal ramps and loss energy. write_multi_period_security / read_multi_period_security preserve results. For external audit, retain the input Study files and campaign contract as well: a result's stored temporal policy is not an independent specification.","title":"Multi-period security"},{"category":"section","location":"mixed_avr_droop/#500-bus-campaign","page":"Mixed AVR / droop","text":"The driver is examples/p2_mixed_avr_500.jl. It writes a fresh campaign and does not resume the paused S2 work. The reference has 90 records: 51 online positive Pmax units, hence 15 AVR and 36 droop units; 39 offline records remain offline.\n\nThe exploratory ratings are explicitly assumed S=Pmax/0.95, not verified source ratings. Fixed AVR targets use nominal S2 saved generator-bus voltages. Droop bounds retain the calibrated 2–8% full-Q-span slopes, reference ±0.02 pu and fixed zero deadband. The equipment policy retains 131 corrective taps and 11 continuous banks with S2 absolute bounds and no extra movement cap. Settled corrective availability is assumed; device delays and discrete bank recovery are unqualified.\n\nThe temporal profile is five one-hour periods at 90/100/105/100/90% demand, scaling both P and Q at every load. Preventive ramps are assumed at 25% of each generator's active capability range per hour. A separate AVR scheduling sensitivity permits ±0.02 pu around the fixed target within normal bus bounds, with ≤0.01 pu/hour movement. The connected 389-outage catalogue and structural island exclusions are reported separately. Security cannot be inferred from a valid base trajectory.\n\nSee the campaign report for actual executed stages, accepted results, failed attempts and outstanding qualification. Numerical failure is not an infeasibility proof. Neither this extension nor its base-operation results close P2.\n\nThe repository campaign ledger, artifacts/p2_mixed_avr_500_20261022/REPORT.md, distinguishes native AC passes, necessary fixed-dispatch exclusions and numerical leftovers. The 14-bus 125-state normal-limit mixed study passes. The 500-bus accepted base profile is not a qualified security-constrained preventive design. The fixed-curve five-period reference is assembled from native independently optimized periods with verified ramps; its bounded coupled-optimizer retries did not qualify.","title":"500-bus campaign"},{"category":"section","location":"mixed_avr_droop/#Reserve-aware-preventive-dispatch","page":"Mixed AVR / droop","text":"Loss-only base dispatch can leave participating generators at Pmax. The optional objective_policy=:reserve_loss inserts a reserve stage after shedding/DR and before loss. For each participating online unit with effective active interval [lo, hi], it imposes Pg + (hi-lo)*h <= hi and maximizes the common h across all periods. The loss stage retains that locally achieved reserve level. objective_policy=:reserve stops after the reserve stage; :loss retains the existing default. This normalized headroom preference is not a generator cost curve, an explicit reserve-delivery guarantee, or an outage-security certificate.\n\nresult = optimize_multi_period_security(studies;\n    droop_controls=controls,\n    objective_policy=:reserve,\n    avr_bounds=avr_bounds,\n    avr_movement_per_hour=0.01,\n    response=:s2_proportional,\n    s2_emergency=true)\n\nReserve stages and their attained values survive JSON round trips. The Julia validator and the independent campaign audit recompute headroom from the physical base states and reject loss polishing that violates the retained reserve level. Numerically zero nonnegative shedding/DR optima are fixed at zero before later stages to avoid a nearly dependent aggregate constraint; failed stricter solves remain failures, not accepted approximations.\n\nFrozen-base assessment may encode different supplied AVR targets in each period, provided every other reactive-assignment field matches. These are fixed inputs to outage evaluation, not new corrective AVR degrees of freedom. Qualify the parent preventive schedule against its bounds and hourly movement limit, then verify that every outage scan uses the same parent targets, curves and base states. Frozen scans do not independently optimize or qualify a new schedule.\n\nThe fresh continuation driver is examples/p2_mixed_avr_reserve.jl; its evidence ledger is artifacts/p2_mixed_avr_reserve_20261023/REPORT.md. Bounded joint masters are followed by exhaustive frozen-candidate AC assessment. All 389 connected outages must pass all five periods for full qualification; subset resource objectives do not establish full-catalogue resource optimality.\n\nAn optional reserve_generator_outages=[...] dispatch target requires each surviving participant to have room for its share of the named lost generator's entire base output plus reserve_margin_pu (default zero), using the declared surviving participation weights and movement caps. It uses effective active bounds and leaves the equipment, circle, voltage and thermal bounds unchanged. This stricter target assumes no relief for active replacement and does not cover changes in network losses; it is not a replacement for an AC outage solve. The campaign contract records this option, and the independent Python audit checks it separately from physics. Result-only replay cannot recover additional input policies that were not retained with the campaign contract.\n\nobjective_policy=:feasibility is available for a separate constrained witness without resource or loss optimization. A feasible shared design is not a resource-optimal design. Keep feasibility, reserve and loss-polishing attempts in separate evidence folders and report which objective actually converged.\n\nFor a joint master with corrective equipment, initial_equipment[t] may include scenarios=Dict(:base => (taps=..., shunts=...), :outage => (...)). Each scenario uses its own coherent equipment starting values; omitted scenarios fall back to the period entry. Unknown scenario keys are rejected. This option changes numerical initialization only; it does not add corrective controls or relax movement budgets, absolute bounds, or preventive sharing constraints.\n\nA complete cross-attempt primal warm start can also supply one dictionary per period through initial_avr_targets, initial_relief and initial_balancing_power. Relief entries are (dr=..., shed=...) vectors over the original loads, keyed by outage ID; AVR targets are keyed by scheduled generator ID. Unknown IDs, nonfinite data and inconsistent targets at a shared regulated location are rejected. Individual starts are clipped to variable bounds; all original physical and temporal constraints are still enforced. The initial state does not become a valid operating point merely because it was retained across attempts. Inspect all constraint residuals, including AVR and capability relations, as well as power balance.","title":"Reserve-aware preventive dispatch"},{"category":"section","location":"mixed_avr_droop/#P2-policy-rerun","page":"Mixed AVR / droop","text":"The current P2 mixed-control rerun uses examples/p2_mixed_limit_aware.jl. It explicitly selects response=:limit_aware and s2_emergency=false, default capability-range participation, original voltage/thermal bounds, and full load without DR/shedding. The effective-range weights equal the former Pmax weights for this particular zero-Pmin data set; saturation redistribution changes the law.\n\nThe selected 131 taps retain their absolute supplied +/-5% envelope and may move at most 0.025 of supplied ratio from each period's preventive base. The 11 banks remain continuous within their legal susceptance envelopes, with a two-section movement budget. Each has one section, so its absolute range is the tighter bound. This is an assumed 120-second settled sensitivity; it does not qualify immediate zero-movement security, manufacturer delays or discrete switching. The mixed 15 AVR/36 droop split, assumed apparent circles, five-hour profile and latest shared curve/AVR scheduling bounds remain in force.\n\nThe additional proportional-share reserve guard and S2-specific rational capacity filter are not applied. Historical exact-S2 drivers and sealed results retain that historical policy. Fresh results are written to artifacts/p2_mixed_limit_aware_20261023; the original S2 study stays paused.\n\nThe earlier, superseded scan passes 386/389 connected outages across all five periods, including all 51 generator outages. Lines 21 and 36 and transformer 562 remain numerically unresolved. The base candidate retains 21.580884% minimum upward headroom and no generator reaches Pmax in any base period; five-hour base loss energy is 178.13534899 MWh. Its AVR schedule passes bounds and movement checks, but full security qualification remains open. See the P2 policy report.","title":"P2 policy rerun"},{"category":"section","location":"mixed_avr_droop/#Targeted-P2-recovery-under-original-limits-(2026-10-23)","page":"Mixed AVR / droop","text":"The targeted recovery found a common five-period design for line 21, line 36 and transformer 562 with no rating, voltage, capability or equipment-budget relaxation. Preventive dispatch, shared droop and the AVR schedule were reoptimized with a feasibility objective. The prior reserve-aware solution supplied initialization; its minimum individual headroom was not retained as a constraint. The selected design has 2.811439% minimum individual headroom and 1083.766 MW aggregate peak Pmax-minus-Pg headroom. The report records the subsequent exhaustive frozen-design validation separately from rejected exploratory solves. This does not establish loss/resource optimality, discrete switching feasibility or transient security.\n\nFinal frozen-design validation: 389/389 connected outages accepted across all five periods, with independent Python audit, unchanged-policy identity and current-source Julia replay of all 390 selected result files. The five-period connected steady-state AVR schedule is qualified within this declared scope.","title":"Targeted P2 recovery under original limits (2026-10-23)"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Data-model","page":"Data Model","text":"","title":"Data model"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Network-and-operating-devices","page":"Data Model","text":"Bus, Branch, Load, and ACNetwork describe the balanced AC network in per-unit quantities. A Generator is attached to a network bus and carries active/reactive limits, availability, and initial values.\n\nnetwork = ACNetwork(\n    [Bus(1; reference = true), Bus(2)],\n    [Branch(1, 1, 2; resistance = 0.01, reactance = 0.1,\n            thermal_limit = 5.0)],\n)\ngenerator = Generator(\n    1, 1;\n    p_min = 0.0, p_max = 2.0,\n    q_min = -1.0, q_max = 1.0,\n    initial_p = 0.5,\n)","title":"Network and operating devices"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Volt-var-droops","page":"Data Model","text":"VoltageSchedule stores the reference voltage and deadband. VoltVarDroop adds a positive voltage-per-reactive-power slope, a reactive reference inside the deadband, and a ReactiveCapability.\n\ncontrol = VoltVarDroop(\n    VoltageSchedule(1.0; v_db_low = 0.99, v_db_high = 1.01),\n    0.05,\n    0.0,\n    ReactiveCapability(p_min = 0.0, p_max = 2.0,\n                       q_min = -1.0, q_max = 1.0),\n)\n\nThe exact curve is available through droop_curve(control) and evaluated with droop_response(control, voltage; p = active_power). The active-power range on the control is enforced for its attached generator.\n\nq_ref(control) is the preferred name for the constant reactive reference inside the voltage deadband. The legacy q_at_deadband field remains available for backward compatibility. New code may use the keyword constructor:\n\ncontrol = VoltVarDroop(schedule, slope, capability; q_ref = 0.0)","title":"Volt-var droops"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Explicit-reactive-control-modes","page":"Data Model","text":"FreeQ, FixedQ, AVR, and VoltVarDroop are explicit ReactiveControlModes. A ReactiveControlAssignment associates one mode with one generator; AVR and Volt–VAr modes also require a regulated location. validate_reactive_assignments rejects duplicate generator assignments, out-of-range fixed-Q schedules and AVR setpoints, unknown locations, and droop capabilities incompatible with generator limits.\n\nassignments = [\n    ReactiveControlAssignment(1, AVR(1.02), RegulatedLocation(:bus, 1)),\n    ReactiveControlAssignment(2, FixedQ(0.1)),\n]\nvalidate_reactive_assignments(case, assignments)\n\nreactive_control_assignments(case) gives every generator in a legacy case an explicit mode without modifying the case: attached droops remain VoltVarDroop, and unattached generators become FreeQ. The existing solver and study-file representation remains backward compatible.\n\nFor base-case smooth or CCOpt OPF, pass reactive_assignments=assignments to solve_opf or solve_opf_complementarity, respectively, and to validate_equilibrium. This list replaces legacy attachments for that call; omitted generators have bounded, free Q. FixedQ adds a Q schedule equality and the independent validator reports :fixed_q when its error exceeds power_tolerance. Unavailable generators retain zero output. AVR is supported by both the smooth and CCOpt base-case entry points. Study JSON v8 and SCOPF result JSON v4 store optional assignments explicitly, along with M9 equipment controls, timing, movement budgets, and scenario settings. Readers preserve nothing for older documents, which retains legacy attachment semantics. Restored assignments are revalidated and SCOPF validation rejects a study/result assignment mismatch. Joint design accepts the same optional assignments for base-case tap/shunt design, validates the returned equilibrium against them, and persists them in joint-design schema v2. Explicit assignments cannot be combined with legacy droop-parameter design variables in one run. When assignments contain only FreeQ/FixedQ, there are no complementarity constraints and CCOpt delegates to MadNLP; these runs are not independent third-solver evidence. Explicit VoltVarDroop assignments retain the exact complementarity encoding.","title":"Explicit reactive-control modes"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Attachments","page":"Data Model","text":"GeneratorControlAttachment connects one generator to one control and gives the regulated location. M1 permits one static attachment per generator.\n\nattachment = GeneratorControlAttachment(\n    1, 1, RegulatedLocation(:bus, 1),\n)\n\nThis explicit attachment layer leaves room for plant-level, remote-bus, and system-level control semantics in later milestones.","title":"Attachments"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#M5.1-transformer-data-contract","page":"Data Model","text":"Branch accepts explicit kind=:line or kind=:transformer, plus tap_ratio=1 and phase_shift=0. The complex tap is on the declared from side: a = tap_ratio * cis(phase_shift). Ratio is dimensionless, finite and strictly positive; phase is finite and in radians. Existing keyword and typed positional constructors retain unity/zero defaults. Network/case conversion, control attachment and contingency copies preserve both settings.\n\nMATPOWER TAP=0 becomes unity and marks a line; a supplied TAP (including unity) or SHIFT marks a transformer. SHIFT is converted once from degrees to radians. No physical tap bounds or legal positions are inferred from those fields. M6.1 imports nonzero bus GS/BS as aggregate fixed shunts (see below).\n\nM5 introduced study schema v2 with both transformer settings required; M6.1 introduced v3 with an explicit fixed-shunt list; M6.2 writes v4 with banks. The reader migrates line-only v1 studies to unity/zero and rejects contradictory transformer metadata labelled v1. Unchanged result/report document kinds retain schema v1. Older readers reject v2, rather than silently losing transformer physics.\n\nStudy schema v9 introduced the explicit line/transformer kind; current v10 also identifies limit-aware automatic active response. Supported older uncoupled or corrective studies reload with kind=:line when kind metadata is absent. Historical coupled preventive studies require original-source replay rather than silent migration to a different active-response policy.\n\nM5.2–M5.4 support fixed ratios and fixed phase shifts in AC evaluation, OPF, SCOPF and existing bounded droop design. Unavailable branches contribute no current while retaining supplied settings. Tap optimization, AVR and switched shunts belong to later milestones.\n\nRun julia --project=. examples/m5_1_transformer_data.jl /tmp/m51-evidence from the repository root to create study JSON, expected/actual numerical evidence, a Markdown report and an SVG showing the from-side convention. The synthetic fixture is not a measured operating point. No AC feasibility is claimed.","title":"M5.1 transformer data contract"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Fixed-transformer-electrical-model-(M5.2–M5.4)","page":"Data Model","text":"For series admittance y = 1/(r + j*x), total branch charging b, and from-side complex tap a = tau * exp(j*phi), the optimizer assembles:\n\nYff = (y + j*b/2) / |a|²       Yft = -y / conj(a)\nYtf = -y / a                  Ytt = y + j*b/2\n\nIt applies |Sf| <= rating and |St| <= rating separately. Both the smooth JuMP formulation (Ipopt/MadNLP) and complementarity formulation (CCOpt) use the same terminal-limit builder, including transformer-outage exclusion. Phase shifts make the off-diagonal entries different; they must not be symmetrized.\n\nPhysical validation follows a separate calculation. With Uf = Vf/a and J = y*(Uf - Vt), it computes If = (J + j*b/2*Uf)/conj(a) and It = -J + j*b/2*Vt, then Sf = Vf*conj(If) and St = Vt*conj(It). Bus balances sum these terminal powers rather than reuse optimizer Ybus. Both terminal powers are positive into the branch; their sum is net branch absorption. Charging is included once at each internal terminal, with the from-side contribution transformed by the tap.\n\nHand-calculated current/power fixtures, scalar polar sweeps, individual terminal overload tests and intentionally corrupted settings check the two implementations. Synthetic OPF/SCOPF studies check exact droop residuals, scenario coupling, outages, JSON replay and preservation under bounded droop optimization. These are steady-state tests, not dynamic or global-optimality certificates.\n\nFor the complete evidence bundle, run from the repository root:\n\nPYTHON=/path/to/python-with-matplotlib julia --project=. examples/m5_transformer_workflow.jl /tmp/m5-evidence\n\nThe Python environment needs Matplotlib (tested with 3.9.4); it is a plotting-only dependency, not a Julia package dependency. The workflow writes numerical JSON, per-solver reports, ratio/phase reference plots, both-terminal loading panels, voltage profiles, exact-droop plots and residual plots. Failed runs remain in the JSON/report and cause a nonzero exit. Existing line-only M1–M4 tests remain part of the regression gate. No three-winding or separate magnetizing branch model is introduced. M6.1 adds MATPOWER aggregate fixed GS/BS; switched-bank data are a separate later capability.\n\nThe M5 evidence declares proportional-regime initial states rather than flat deadband starts. The latter can stall even on a feasible study. For CCOpt, the example requests ProportionalRelaxationUpdate(sigma_min=1e-12), tol=acceptable_tol=1e-9 and bound_relax_factor=0; its default relaxation floor left exact-droop errors above the 1e-5 acceptance tolerance. These are example-specific solver settings, not equipment parameters or relaxed acceptance criteria. Diagnostic failures are retained with the evidence.","title":"Fixed transformer electrical model (M5.2–M5.4)"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Fixed-bus-shunts-(M6.1)","page":"Data Model","text":"FixedShunt is network equipment, separate from Load and branch charging:\n\nshunt = FixedShunt(8, 10; conductance=0.02, susceptance=0.1, available=true)\nnetwork = ACNetwork([Bus(10; reference=true)], Branch[]; shunts=[shunt])\n\nIDs and bus references must be positive; IDs are unique within the shunt list. Admittance is finite and in per unit on the case base. Conductance G must be nonnegative; susceptance B may have either sign. Positive B is capacitive. Complex terminal consumption is S = (G - j*B)*|V|²: positive P consumes active power, negative Q injects reactive power. Several distinct devices may share a bus and their contributions sum once. available=false contributes zero while preserving supplied data. An empty list preserves the previous network model.\n\nThe optimizer adds G+j*B to the bus diagonal in Ybus. The independent evaluator shunt_powers(network, state) computes V*conj((G+j*B)*V) per device in network.shunts order. power_balance sums these powers alongside branch terminal powers. Bus shunts do not enter branch_flows or duplicate line charging.\n\nMATPOWER GS is MW consumed at unit voltage; BS is MVAr injected at unit voltage. Import divides both by baseMVA, with no sign reversal: G=GS/baseMVA, B=BS/baseMVA. Nonzero aggregate rows become one fixed record using the bus ID as shunt ID; zero rows add none. No bank identity, legal step, nominal schedule or automatic controller is inferred. Negative GS is rejected because this passive equipment model does not represent active-power injection.\n\nStudy JSON v3 requires network.shunts; each record includes ID, bus ID, G, B and availability. Readers retain v1/v2 support with empty shunts and reject nonempty shunts misleadingly labelled with an older schema. New writers use v4 even when the list is empty, so old readers fail explicitly. Other document kinds keep their existing versions. Numeric promotion, case/control copies and existing branch/generator contingency overlays preserve fixed shunts. Dedicated shunt contingencies are not introduced in M6.1.\n\nRun the evidence workflow with Python/Matplotlib available:\n\nPYTHON=/path/to/python-with-matplotlib julia --project=. examples/m6_1_fixed_shunts.jl /tmp/m61-evidence\n\nIt generates a GS/BS conversion table, expected/computed P(V) and Q(V) curves, availability and round-trip checks, and deliberate omission/double-count/sign errors. A one-bus OPF checks safe use of the shared Ybus path. Switched-bank states (M6.2) and the complete OPF/SCOPF comparison bundle (M6.3) are implemented; see archived M6 evidence.","title":"Fixed bus shunts (M6.1)"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Supplied-switched-bank-states-(M6.2–M6.3)","page":"Data Model","text":"bank = ShuntBank(201, 30;\n    step_susceptances=(0.02, -0.01),\n    step_conductances=(0.001, 0.0005),\n    legal_states=((0,0), (1,0), (2,0), (0,1), (1,1)),\n    state=(1,0), nominal_state=(1,0))\nchanged = with_bank_state(bank, (2,0))\nnetwork = ACNetwork(buses, branches; shunts=fixed_shunts, banks=[changed])\n\nEach state is a tuple of nonnegative integer counts for the corresponding step admittances. Legal combinations must be explicitly supplied, nonempty and unique; both current and nominal states must be legal even when the bank is unavailable. Metadata are copied into immutable tuples. Unavailability produces zero admittance without erasing the installed state. bank_admittance returns aggregate G+jB; bank_powers independently computes consumption P+jQ by summing step currents. Positive B is capacitive, so Q is negative consumption. Fixed shunts and banks share a unique ID namespace. Do not represent the same physical aggregate in both. MATPOWER GS/BS remains fixed admittance and does not infer bank identities or states.\n\nStudy schema v4 requires network.banks and preserves all metadata. Schema v5 adds optional reactive-control assignments; schema v6 adds branch_id to a branch-terminal location; schema v7 adds selected continuous equipment and its SCOPF timing policy; schema v8 adds optional corrective movement-limit maps. Readers migrate v1-v3 to empty banks and reject nonempty banks mislabeled as old schemas. Existing branch/generator contingencies preserve supplied bank states. M6 uses fixed states in OPF, preventive/corrective SCOPF and droop design. Optimization of equipment is M7; a bank state is not an automatic switching policy or trajectory.","title":"Supplied switched-bank states (M6.2–M6.3)"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Continuous-transformer-tap-design-(M7.1)","page":"Data Model","text":"policy = [TapControl(11; lower=0.95, upper=1.05, nominal=1.0)]\nresult = optimize_taps(case, policy; initial_state=state, smooth_epsilon=1e-5)\nreport = validate_tap_design(case, result)\nphysical_case = with_tap_settings(case, result.taps)\nmetrics = tap_design_metrics(case, result)\nwrite_tap_design(\"tap-design.json\", result)\nreloaded = read_tap_design(\"tap-design.json\")\n\nBounds are required; no range is inferred from imported taps. Branch IDs must be unique, known and available. Omitted branches remain at their supplied ratios. Set lower == upper to fix a selected device, and supply initial if the supplied ratio is outside the bounds. Initial values are numerical starts; nominal is a positive reference for reporting, defaulting to the supplied ratio, and need not lie in the optimization envelope. Neither introduces a penalty in the objective.\n\nThe baseline objective remains generator dispatch deviation. Phase shifts, shunts and droop curves stay fixed. Ratios enter nonlinear balances and both-terminal thermal constraints; input case data remain unchanged. Results are continuous relaxations, without discrete-position recovery or switching-count interpretation. report.physical evaluates the reconstructed physical network; policy_valid separately checks bounds and fixed settings. A failed solve can have no state or ratios; physical validation is then unavailable and overall validity is false.\n\nThe dedicated base-case entry point accepts a Case. M9.1 also accepts the same controls on Study, with explicit scenario policy coupling through SCOPFControlPolicy; see SCOPF. The current implementation uses the smooth encoding with Ipopt by default and supports the existing optimizer-factory interface. See M7.1 evidence for sweeps and comparisons.","title":"Continuous transformer tap design (M7.1)"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Simple-continuous-shunt-design-(M7.2)","page":"Data Model","text":"bank = ShuntBank(201, 30; step_susceptances=(0.02,),\n    step_conductances=(0.001,), legal_states=((0,), (1,), (2,), (3,)), state=(1,))\n# After adding bank to case.network.banks:\npolicy = [ShuntControl(201)] # B bounds default to [0, 0.06] pu\nresult = optimize_shunts(case, policy; initial_state=state)\nreport = validate_shunt_design(case, result)\nmetrics = shunt_design_metrics(case, result)\nphysical_case = with_shunt_settings(case, result.susceptances)\nwrite_shunt_design(\"shunt-design.json\", result)\nreloaded = read_shunt_design(\"shunt-design.json\")\n\nA reactor uses negative step B, e.g. −0.02 gives an interval [−0.06, 0]. G is tied to B through their common fractional count; here G=0.001(B/Bstep). Banks with multiple step types or zero step B are rejected when selected, as are unavailable or unknown banks and fixed-shunt IDs. Unselected heterogeneous banks remain fixed.\n\nOptional lower, upper, initial and nominal are all B in pu. Bounds must lie in the legal-count envelope; equal bounds fix B. The default initial B is the supplied bank setting, so narrowing bounds past it requires an explicit start. The default nominal B comes from the bank's nominal state. Nominal B must lie in the physical envelope but may lie outside a narrower optimization interval.\n\nTaps, phase shifts and droop settings stay fixed; multiple simple banks can be selected independently. The baseline dispatch objective remains unchanged. Metrics include fractional count, B deviations, G, active consumption, reactive injection and branch losses. The result is explicitly continuous, not a recovered switching position. with_shunt_settings creates an evaluation case by replacing selected banks with equivalent fixed shunts; the original case retains bank metadata. Policy bounds are checked separately by validate_shunt_design. JSON stores settings and policy; retain the input study alongside it for reproducible replay.\n\nValidation evidence includes capacitor/reactor sweeps and plots. Heterogeneous optimization is post-scaling backlog; the gate requires evidence that simple banks, transformers and droops scale adequately first.","title":"Simple continuous shunt design (M7.2)"},{"category":"section","location":"data_model/#Joint-equipment-and-droop-design-(M7.3)","page":"Data Model","text":"result = optimize_joint_design(case;\n    tap_controls=[TapControl(11; lower=0.95, upper=1.05)],\n    shunt_controls=[ShuntControl(201)],\n    droop_controls=[DroopControl(2; slope_bounds=(0.04, 0.10))],\n    initial_state=state)\nreport = validate_joint_design(case, result)\nphysical_case = with_joint_settings(case, result)\nmetrics = joint_design_metrics(case, result)\nwrite_joint_design(\"joint-design.json\", result)\nreloaded = read_joint_design(\"joint-design.json\")\n\nOmit a control list to keep that family fixed; select devices independently within each list. DroopControl additionally accepts v_ref_bounds, deadband_low_bounds, deadband_high_bounds and initial::DroopSettings. Missing bounds fix a parameter to its supplied value. Bounds preserve the M3 positive-slope, positive-reference and positive-total-deadband contract. No free-knot curve family is introduced. Multiple attached available droop controls may be selected.\n\nThe objective is identical across fixed/free configurations. Reports distinguish solver success, parameter/equipment policy compliance and reconstructed physical validity. Ratios and bank settings remain continuous relaxations. See M7 results and visualizations for the complete grid, multi-start variability, objective components and matched conditional benefits.","title":"Joint equipment and droop design (M7.3)"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Getting-started","page":"Getting Started","text":"","title":"Getting started"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Installation","page":"Getting Started","text":"DroopOPF.jl currently installs from GitHub:\n\nimport Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/frederikgeth/DroopOPF.jl.git\", rev = \"v0.10.0-rc1\")\n\nFor a checkout, instantiate the project from the repository root:\n\njulia --project=. -e 'using Pkg; Pkg.instantiate()'\n\nJulia 1.10 or newer is supported by the package project.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Solve-a-case","page":"Getting Started","text":"Create a Case from an ACNetwork, Loads, Generators, VoltVarDroop controls, and GeneratorControlAttachments. A complete runnable two-bus example is available at examples/m1_regime_case_study.jl.\n\nusing DroopOPF\n\n# `case` is a fully assembled DroopOPF.Case.\nresult = solve_opf(case; smooth_epsilon = 1.0e-3)\nreport = equilibrium_report(case, result)\nprintln(markdown_report(report))\n\nThe result contains the solved ACState, objective, solver statuses, and the smoothing parameters used by the model.","title":"Solve a case"},{"category":"section","location":"getting_started/#Inspect-operating-points","page":"Getting Started","text":"points = droop_operating_points(case, result.state)\nfor point in points\n    println(point.generator_id, \": \", point.regime,\n            \" V=\", point.voltage, \" Q=\", point.reactive_power)\nend\n\nwrite_droop_plot(\"droop_operating_points.svg\", case, result.state)","title":"Inspect operating points"},{"category":"section","location":"multi_period/#P3-multi-period-droop-design","page":"Multi Period","text":"Historical artifacts/ paths on this page identify local campaign records, not files bundled with the release. See archive access and the current qualification index.\n\nThe initial multi-period capability solves one deterministic sequence of AC operating periods in a single sparse nonlinear model. A single set of reactive voltage-droop parameters is shared across all periods. Each period has its own active/reactive dispatch and voltage state. Equipment stays at the supplied fixed settings throughout the horizon.\n\nThis is an initial implementation of P3-04. P2 remains open, and the original S2 campaign remains paused. Multi-period outage recourse, storage, commitment, equipment scheduling and dynamic trajectories are separate extensions.","title":"P3 multi-period droop design"},{"category":"section","location":"multi_period/#Time-and-objective-contract","page":"Multi Period","text":"Cases must share the same ordered network, equipment settings, generators, controls, attachments, power base, frequency and load identities. Only load powers may vary. All original voltage, thermal and generator limits remain active, with optional explicit branch-angle bounds.\n\nFor period t, duration_hours[t] is both its energy weight and the elapsed start-to-start time to period t+1. The last duration weights energy only. There is no initial or terminal ramp boundary, cyclic closure or interpolated trajectory. Inputs represent period operating points with a piecewise-constant energy accounting convention.\n\nThe objective is the sum of duration_hours[t] * base_power * (sum(pg[t]) - sum(load_p[t])), in MWh. This is active network energy loss, including shunt conductance losses. It is not generation cost or a storage energy balance.\n\nFor generator g, ramp_limits[g] = (up, down) specifies nonnegative rates in pu/hour on the case power base. Between consecutive period starts:\n\n-r_g^downDelta t le P_gt+1-P_gt le r_g^upDelta t\n\nOmitted generators have no inter-period ramp restriction. Zero rates freeze active output across periods. These planned dispatch ramps do not replace the library's automatic contingency-response policy and do not establish dynamic frequency performance.","title":"Time and objective contract"},{"category":"section","location":"multi_period/#API","page":"Multi Period","text":"study = MultiPeriodStudy(period_cases;\n    duration_hours=[1., 1., 2.],\n    ramp_limits=Dict(generator_id => (0.2, 0.1)),\n    angle_limits=Dict(branch_id => (-0.5, 0.5)))\nresult = optimize_multi_period(study;\n    droop_controls=[DroopControl(1;\n        slope_bounds=(0.02, 0.08), v_ref_bounds=(0.98, 1.02))],\n    initial_states=period_initial_states)\naudit = validate_multi_period(study, result)\n\nSlope bounds are in physical pu-voltage / pu-reactive-power units, not percent. Omitted droop fields remain fixed. Empty droop_controls fixes all curves. The first implementation uses the existing smooth volt-var family; the normal independent validator also checks exact PWL droop residuals. Native convergence, all AC states, design bounds, ramp constraints, branch-angle constraints, period losses and the total energy objective must pass independently. Solver-reported local optima do not establish global optimality or design uniqueness.","title":"API"},{"category":"section","location":"multi_period/#Initial-network-campaign","page":"Multi Period","text":"Run into a fresh output directory:\n\nOPENBLAS_NUM_THREADS=1 julia --project=. examples/p3_multi_period.jl /fresh/output\npython3 -B examples/audit_p3_multi_period.py /fresh/output\npython3 examples/plot_p3_multi_period.py /fresh/output\n\nThe plotting command requires Matplotlib and NumPy. The Python audit replays complex-current AC physics separately from the Julia optimization implementation. Inputs, warm starts, policies, source hashes, native statuses and every state are saved by the example. It compares supplied fixed curves and shared designed curves under identical profiles and equipment settings.\n\nThe 22 October 2026 campaign uses five one-hour periods:\n\nNetwork Ordered demand factors Fixed curves, MWh Shared design, MWh Loss reduction\nSynthetic 3-bus 90, 110, 95, 100, 90% 2.74757 2.70002 1.73%\nARPA-E S1 14-bus 90, 100, 110, 100, 90% 3.31966 2.84483 14.30%\nARPA-E S1 500-bus 90, 100, 105, 100, 90% 158.09748 83.11411 47.43%\n\nAll six solves terminated LOCALLY_SOLVED; Julia validation and independent Python replay accepted all 30 period states. These are finite sampled profiles, not continuous demand-range or N−1 qualification. No emergency limits, demand response or shedding are used. Network data retain S1's Pmin=0 and symmetric-Q study assumptions.\n\nAll taps and banks remain fixed at the S1 supplied reference settings. Banks retain continuous reference susceptance values; no discrete switching claim is made. Generator ramps are assumed at 25% of active capability range per hour; the synthetic generator 7 instead has zero movement to exercise coupling. They are sensitivity assumptions, not verified manufacturer ramp data. Droop slopes/references are shared across all five periods: the public cases use 2–8% voltage span across the full reactive capability and reference ±0.02 pu; the synthetic case uses 0.5–2 times supplied slopes and reference ±0.02 pu. Deadband widths remain supplied and fixed.\n\nDetailed evidence and three figures are retained in artifacts/p3_multi_period_20261022_v2/REPORT.md. The large percentage reduction is relative to the supplied fixed curves under the same ramp constraints; it is not a comparison with independently retuned hourly curves or a global bound.","title":"Initial network campaign"},{"category":"section","location":"multi_period/#Mixed-AVR-and-contingency-recourse-extension","page":"Multi Period","text":"The base-operation API above retains its fixed-equipment scope. The separate optimize_multi_period_security API supports mixed AVR/droop controls, apparent capability circles, outage recourse, explicit Study equipment policies and optional preventive AVR scheduling. See Mixed AVR and droop studies for the model contract, declared S2 comparison settings and qualification limits.","title":"Mixed AVR and contingency recourse extension"},{"category":"section","location":"validation_evidence/#Selected-validation-references","page":"Validation references","text":"These compact references illustrate numerical verification of the implemented steady-state equations and declared study policies. They are not field validation, a guarantee for arbitrary networks, or certification of dynamic behavior. Full research archives remain local. No large network inputs or solver logs are included here.","title":"Selected validation references"},{"category":"section","location":"validation_evidence/#Transformer-optimization:-a-small,-interpretable-check","page":"Validation references","text":"(Image: Synthetic three-bus tap sweep)\n\nThis historical M7.1 sweep compares fixed and optimized continuous taps on a synthetic three-bus system, with phase shifts, shunts and droops fixed. The objective is dispatch deviation, not loss minimization. The fixed / one-free / two-free tap solutions have maximum AC residuals of 2.43e-15 / 2.87e-13 / 5.83e-14 pu. See the equipment report tables for settings, objectives, acceptance tolerances and limitations. The figure is copied unchanged from the archived report; its source path and SHA-256 are in the summary below. A sweep supports this bounded comparison, not global optimality in larger models.","title":"Transformer optimization: a small, interpretable check"},{"category":"section","location":"validation_evidence/#Independent-replay-of-the-recovered-500-bus-joint-model","page":"Validation references","text":"(Image: Julia and Python physical residuals)\n\nEach point is the largest residual among the base and outages 21, 36 and 562 at that period. There are 20 states in this selected joint model. Julia and independent Python replay evaluate the saved solution; the plot does not represent every state in the subsequent full catalogue. Its small residuals demonstrate numerical agreement with the equations. Acceptance also checks voltage, thermal and capability limits, AVR behavior, response and equipment policy; those checks cannot be replaced by a residual plot.","title":"Independent replay of the recovered 500-bus joint model"},{"category":"section","location":"validation_evidence/#Preventive-AVR-schedule","page":"Validation references","text":"(Image: Five-period AVR schedule)\n\nThe schedule is optimized jointly with dispatch and shared droop curves, then frozen across outages within each period. Fifteen generators use AVR and 36 use generic volt-var droop. Maximum hourly target movement is 0.00052050 pu, below the declared 0.01 pu/hour budget. Targets stay within nominal +/-0.02 pu clipped to normal voltage bounds. These are steady-state operating setpoints, not a simulation of excitation dynamics.","title":"Preventive AVR schedule"},{"category":"section","location":"validation_evidence/#Compact-campaign-report-—-23-October-2026","page":"Validation references","text":"Check Recorded result\nCandidate / objective joint-five; feasibility\nNetwork / profile 500 buses; five one-hour periods at 90/100/105/100/90% demand\nConnected outage coverage 389/389 accepted five-period trajectories\nRemaining numerical failures None in this connected catalogue\nFinal Julia replay 390 selected result files; 3,910 state instances including repeated bases\nIndependent Python audit Selected trajectories and frozen-policy identity passed\nLoad service / operating limits Full load; no DR or shedding; normal voltage and original Rate A\nActive response Limit-aware capability-range participation with saturation redistribution\nCapability Existing P/Q boxes intersected with assumed S=Pmax/0.95 circles\nCorrective equipment 131 continuous taps, supplied +/-5% absolute bounds, <=0.025 supplied-ratio movement; 11 continuous banks within legal envelopes and <=two sections\nTiming Assumed 120-second settled sensitivity; not verified manufacturer timing\nPhysical/control limit relaxation None\nFive-hour base loss energy 121.62343825 MWh; not a certified minimum-loss result\n\nThe preceding candidate left outages 21, 36 and 562 numerically unresolved. Reoptimizing preventive dispatch, shared curves and AVR targets recovered them; it did not establish that their previous residuals proved physical infeasibility. Rejected expanded masters and failed attempts remain in the full local report. Islanding, dynamic security, discrete implementation, detailed GFL/GFM behavior and full-catalogue resource/loss optimality are outside this qualification. Original S2 remains paused and broader P2 is not closed.","title":"Compact campaign report — 23 October 2026"},{"category":"section","location":"validation_evidence/#Provenance-and-reproduction","page":"Validation references","text":"Download the compact evidence summary. It includes the plotted residuals and AVR targets, coverage counts, and SHA-256 identities of the source report, result, replay records and sealed manifest. Source campaign: artifacts/p2_targeted_relaxations_20261023. The hashes identify local originals; they do not substitute for publishing those originals or enable full independent reproduction from this small summary alone.\n\nRegenerate the two new plots from the bundled summary, with Python and matplotlib:\n\npython3 tools/release/plot_validation_references.py\n\nThe M7 image is an unchanged historical figure. Full campaign reproduction needs the locally archived inputs and evidence, appropriate solver versions and computational resources. These documentation plots do not rerun optimization or change any sealed result.\n\nSoftware validation is separate: v0.10.0-rc1 passed 3,596 assertions on Julia 1.10/Linux, 1.12/Linux and 1.12/macOS, plus documentation and clean-install checks. Those are release-time records, not newly executed campaign results.","title":"Provenance and reproduction"},{"category":"section","location":"validation/#Validation","page":"Validation","text":"Solver termination is not, by itself, proof that a generator operating point is physically valid. validate_equilibrium recomputes the AC power balance, generator limits, branch margins, and exact droop residuals independently.\n\nFor acceptance of a droop replacement, use the feasibility precursor, which also freezes equipment freedom, optional source angle limits and tolerances, and distinguishes an unresolved search from certified infeasibility.\n\nvalidation = validate_equilibrium(case, result.state)\nvalidation.valid\nvalidation.power_balance_max\nvalidation.droop_residual_max\n\nFor a smooth result, passing the result object also records the smoothing gap:\n\nreport = equilibrium_report(case, result)\nprintln(markdown_report(report))\n\nThe report checks:\n\nmaximum active/reactive AC power-balance residual;\nexact generator droop residual;\nvoltage, active-power, reactive-power, and thermal margins;\ncontrol active-power-range violations;\nsmooth-versus-exact droop gap, when applicable.\n\nFor cross-solver tests, start with 1e-5 per-unit AC residuals, 5e-4 per-unit exact droop residuals, and 1e-5 complementarity products. Tighten these after scaling and conditioning have been characterized on larger cases.\n\nSee the milestone index and selected validation figures and reports.","title":"Validation"},{"category":"section","location":"#DroopOPF.jl","page":"Index","text":"AC OPF, reactive control design and independently validated steady-state SCOPF.\n\nStart with installation, release scope, SCOPF, multi-period operation, mixed controls or PSS/E.\n\nHistorical study reports and figures are in the optional research archives.\n\nSee the milestone index and selected validation figures and reports.","title":"DroopOPF.jl"}]
}
